{"type":"agent_end","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read prompt.md and complete the exercise following the workflow rules. Continue autonomously through ALL tests in the test list until you have written experiment-done.txt with the single word DONE. Do NOT stop after a single passing test or cycle — keep going until every test is implemented."}],"timestamp":1784951298915},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me start by reading the prompt.md file to understand the exercise.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_3fa131bcf3e64eba972a6dbc","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"prompt.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":2776,"output":26,"cacheRead":1792,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":15,"totalTokens":4594,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951298935,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-36caccae-edf6-40f4-8559-b04e437b1702","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_3fa131bcf3e64eba972a6dbc","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# MHPCO Claim Office Kata\n\n## Setting\n\nYou are building the policy management system for the **Most Honorable\nPrivileged Claims Office for Magical Risks and Cursed Items** (MHPCO),\nan old-fashioned insurance institution that covers magical items —\nswords, amulets, staves, potions, and components such as runes and\nmoonstones. The MHPCO is bureaucratic, slightly stingy, and proud of\nits venerable traditions.\n\n## Feature\n\nThe system supports two operations: **quote** computes the premium for\na list of items a customer wishes to insure, and **claim** processes a\ndamage report against an existing policy.\n\n## Rules\n\n### Item values and base premiums\n\nEach main item has an insurance value and a base premium according to\nthe MHPCO price list:\n\n- Sword: 1000 G insurance value, 100 G base premium\n- Amulet: 600 G / 60 G\n- Staff: 800 G / 80 G\n- Potion: 400 G / 40 G\n\nComponents — for example runes and moonstones — are insured at 250 G\neach, with a base premium of 25 G per component. A building block of\n3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\n\n### Premium modifiers\n\n- Cursed items add a 50 % risk surcharge.\n- Highly enchanted items (enchantment level ≥ 5) add a 30 % risk\n  surcharge.\n- Long-standing customers (≥ 2 years of business with MHPCO) receive a\n  20 % loyalty discount.\n- A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\n- Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\n- A 5 G processing fee is added to every premium.\n\nAll amounts are rounded to whole G in the MHPCO's favor.\n\n### Claim processing\n\nA deductible of 100 G applies per damage event. The total payout per\npolicy is capped at twice the insurance sum.\n\n- Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50 % of\n  the damage amount.\n- Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\n\n## Examples\n\nThe examples below illustrate single rules in isolation. Values shown\nare base premiums or raw payout amounts before integration with other\nmodifiers, unless stated otherwise.\n\n### Building block of 3 alike components\n\n- 2 runes → 50 G base premium\n- 3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\n- 4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\n- 7 runes → 175 G base premium\n\n### \"Alike\" components\n\n> ❓ Hmm — \"alike\" components: does that mean exactly the same type,\n> or just the same family (rune-y, gemstone-y)?\n\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block: different types)\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\n\n### Modifier scope on multi-item policies\n\n> ❓ A policy with several items: does the cursed surcharge apply to\n> the whole policy, or just to the cursed item?\n\n- a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain\n  amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed\n  surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not\n  of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\n- item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the\n  base premium of the affected item; policy-wide modifiers (loyalty,\n  first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base\n  premium (the sum of all item base premiums); the processing fee is\n  added at the very end\n\n### Modifier thresholds\n\n- customer with exactly 2 years with MHPCO → loyalty discount applies\n- sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge\n  applies; if cursed, both surcharges apply\n- sword with enchantment 4 → no high-enchantment surcharge; curse\n  surcharge applies only if cursed\n- dragon-material sword with exactly enchantment 8, damage 1000 G →\n  payout 400 G (high-enchantment clause applies, then deductible)\n\n### Deductible per damage event\n\n- a dragon attack damages an insured sword (500 G) and an insured\n  amulet (300 G); payout = 600 G (the 100 G deductible applies once\n  per damaged item)\n\n### Standard reimbursement (no special clauses)\n\n- regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 G → payout 400 G\n  (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies)\n- damage to a rune (insurance value 250 G), damage 200 G → payout\n  100 G (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; runes have no\n  enchantment level or material, so no special clause applies)\n\n### Enchantment threshold vs. dragon material\n\n- dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G\n  (both clauses apply; the 50 % rule wins, then deductible: 500 − 100)\n- dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 G → payout 700 G\n  (only the dragon-material clause applies: full reimbursement,\n  then deductible: 800 − 100)\n- steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G\n  (only the high-enchantment clause applies: 50 % first, then\n  deductible: 500 − 100)\n\n### Multiple items of the same type\n\n> ❓ What if a customer brings two of the same item — one in hand,\n> one in the backpack? Or just two swords because they fight with\n> two?\n\n- a policy covers two swords → insurance sum 2000 G (= 2×1000),\n  cap 4000 G\n- a dragon attack damages both swords; `damages` contains two\n  `{itemType: \"sword\", ...}` entries → each entry is treated as a\n  separate damage with its own deductible\n- if the `damages` array contains more entries of a given type than\n  the policy actually covers (e.g. two sword damages but only one\n  sword insured) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code; the\n  whole claim is rejected\n\n### Cap exhaustion\n\n- a policy covers a sword and an amulet → insurance sum 1600 G\n  (= 1000 + 600, the sum of the items' insurance values), cap 3200 G\n- a cursed sword (insurance value 1000 G, premium with modifiers\n  165 G) → cap 2000 G (based on the unmodified insurance value;\n  premium modifiers do not raise the cap)\n- a policy covers a sword and 3 runes (a block) → insurance sum\n  1750 G (= 1000 + 3×250); the block discount affects the premium\n  only, not the insurance sum\n- a sword is insured (insurance sum 1000 G, cap 2000 G); two successive\n  claims of 1500 G each\n- first claim → payout 1400 G, cap remaining 600 G\n- second claim → payout 600 G, cap remaining 0 G (the desired 1400 G\n  is reduced to the remaining cap)\n\n### Rounding in the MHPCO's favor\n\n- a premium calculation that yields 197.5 G → final premium 198 G\n  (rounded up)\n- a payout calculation that yields 350.5 G → final payout 350 G\n  (rounded down)\n- intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions;\n  only the final premium or payout is rounded\n\n### Edge cases\n\n- empty item list → premium 5 G (only the processing fee)\n- quote includes an item with an unknown type (e.g.\n  `{type: \"broomstick\"}`) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\n  and writes an error description to stderr; no `results` are written\n  to stdout\n- claim references a damage entry whose item is not part of the policy\n  (e.g. an amulet damaged when only a sword is insured, or an item\n  with an unknown type) → the CLI exits with a non-zero status code\n  and writes an error description to stderr\n- claim contains a damage entry with `amount: -200` → the CLI exits\n  with a non-zero status code and writes an error description to\n  stderr\n\n## Integration examples\n\nThe following two examples combine multiple rules to clarify how\nmodifiers stack and how customer history affects the premium.\n\n### Newcomer with a cursed sword\n\n- customer: 0 years with MHPCO, no previous contract\n- item: a cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3)\n- premium: 165 G\n- (100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G)\n\n### Long-standing customer's second contract\n\n> ❓ Wait — does \"first insurance\" mean the customer's first ever\n> contract, or the first time we see this particular item? A\n> long-standing customer with a brand-new sword: does the first\n> insurance surcharge still apply?\n\n- customer: 3 years with MHPCO; this is the customer's second `quote`\n  in the scenario\n- item: a cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7)\n- premium: 160 G\n- (100 G base + 50 G curse + 30 G high enchantment − 20 G loyalty\n  + 10 G first insurance − 15 G follow-up contract = 155 G + 5 G fee\n  = 160 G)\n- The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even\n  though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a\n  `quote` is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer\n  history.\n\n## CLI input/output format\n\nThe implementation must be exposed as a command-line executable\n`claim-office`. It reads a JSON document from stdin describing a\nscenario and writes a JSON document with the corresponding results to\nstdout.\n\nA scenario is a JSON object with a `customer` object and a `steps`\narray. The `customer` describes the single customer for the whole\nscenario. Each step has an `op` field (`\"quote\"` or `\"claim\"`) and the\noperation-specific input fields. Steps are processed sequentially;\nlater `claim` steps refer to a policy created by an earlier `quote`\nstep via its zero-based step index in a `policy` field.\n\nStdout receives a JSON object with a `results` array of the same length\nand order as the input `steps`. Each result contains:\n\n- For a `quote` step: `premium` (integer, total premium in G).\n- For a `claim` step: `payout` (integer, payout in G) and\n  `remainingCap` (integer, cap remaining on the policy after this\n  claim).\n\n### JSON Schema (normative)\n\nThe field names below are **binding**. Your implementation must use\nexactly these names; do not rename or restructure them.\n\n**Input (stdin):**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"customer\", \"steps\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"customer\": {\n      \"type\": \"object\",\n      \"required\": [\"yearsWithMHPCO\"],\n      \"properties\": {\n        \"yearsWithMHPCO\": { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n      }\n    },\n    \"steps\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"oneOf\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Quote step\",\n            \"required\": [\"op\", \"items\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"op\": { \"const\": \"quote\" },\n              \"items\": {\n                \"type\": \"array\",\n                \"items\": {\n                  \"type\": \"object\",\n                  \"required\": [\"type\"],\n                  \"properties\": {\n                    \"type\":        { \"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"sword | amulet | staff | potion | rune | moonstone\" },\n                    \"material\":    { \"type\": \"string\" },\n                    \"enchantment\": { \"type\": \"integer\" },\n                    \"cursed\":      { \"type\": \"boolean\" }\n                  }\n                }\n              }\n            }\n          },\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Claim step\",\n            \"required\": [\"op\", \"policy\", \"incident\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"op\":     { \"const\": \"claim\" },\n              \"policy\": { \"type\": \"integer\", \"description\": \"zero-based index of the quote step that created the policy\" },\n              \"incident\": {\n                \"type\": \"object\",\n                \"required\": [\"cause\", \"damages\"],\n                \"properties\": {\n                  \"cause\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n                  \"damages\": {\n                    \"type\": \"array\",\n                    \"items\": {\n                      \"type\": \"object\",\n                      \"required\": [\"itemType\", \"amount\"],\n                      \"properties\": {\n                        \"itemType\": { \"type\": \"string\" },\n                        \"amount\":   { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n                      }\n                    }\n                  }\n                }\n              }\n            }\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Output (stdout):**\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"object\",\n  \"required\": [\"results\"],\n  \"properties\": {\n    \"results\": {\n      \"type\": \"array\",\n      \"items\": {\n        \"oneOf\": [\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Quote result\",\n            \"required\": [\"premium\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"premium\": { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n            }\n          },\n          {\n            \"description\": \"Claim result\",\n            \"required\": [\"payout\", \"remainingCap\"],\n            \"properties\": {\n              \"payout\":       { \"type\": \"integer\" },\n              \"remainingCap\": { \"type\": \"integer\" }\n            }\n          }\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Schema example\n\nStdin:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"customer\": {\"yearsWithMHPCO\": 5},\n  \"steps\": [\n    {\n      \"op\": \"quote\",\n      \"items\": [\n        {\"type\": \"amulet\", \"material\": \"silver\", \"enchantment\": 2, \"cursed\": false}\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"op\": \"claim\",\n      \"policy\": 0,\n      \"incident\": {\n        \"cause\": \"fire\",\n        \"damages\": [\n          {\"itemType\": \"amulet\", \"amount\": 200}\n        ]\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nStdout shape:\n\n```json\n{\"results\": [{\"premium\": <integer>}, {\"payout\": <integer>, \"remainingCap\": <integer>}]}\n```\n\n## Expected Output Files\n\n- Source files implementing the logic and the `claim-office` CLI\n- Test files\n- CLI entry point at `src/cli.ts` (reads JSON from stdin, writes JSON to stdout)\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Use TypeScript for the implementation\n- The CLI must read JSON from stdin and write JSON to stdout\n- The CLI entry point must be at `src/cli.ts`\n- Internal data structures and module organization are free\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951299543},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"This is a complex kata. Let me first explore the project structure to understand what's already set up.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_18cd03f190394f72a21c1b6e","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la && echo \"---\" && cat package.json 2>/dev/null && echo \"---\" && cat tsconfig.json 2>/dev/null"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":3339,"output":61,"cacheRead":4544,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":22,"totalTokens":7944,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951299544,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-2e459e93-6455-44cc-9c39-01132aa0871d","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_18cd03f190394f72a21c1b6e","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 420\ndrwxr-xr-x    5 experimenter experimenter   4096 Jul 25 03:48 .\ndrwxrwxr-x 1010 experimenter experimenter 159744 Jul 25 03:48 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x    5 experimenter experimenter   4096 Jul 25 03:48 .pi\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter   9993 Jul 25 03:48 AGENTS.md\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter   1568 Jul 25 03:48 eslint.config.mjs\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter    366 Jul 25 03:48 metrics.json\ndrwxr-xr-x    3 experimenter experimenter   4096 Jul 25 03:48 node_modules\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter    537 Jul 25 03:48 package.json\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter  79090 Jul 25 03:48 pnpm-lock.yaml\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter  13225 Jul 25 03:48 prompt.md\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter 117608 Jul 25 03:48 run.log\ndrwxr-xr-x    2 experimenter experimenter   4096 Jul 25 03:48 src\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter    236 Jul 25 03:48 tsconfig.json\n-rw-r--r--    1 experimenter experimenter    306 Jul 25 03:48 vitest.config.ts\n---\n{\n  \"name\": \"tdd-experiment-run\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"test\": \"vitest run\",\n    \"test:unit:basic\": \"vitest run\",\n    \"test:watch\": \"vitest\",\n    \"test:coverage\": \"vitest run --coverage --coverage.reporter=json-summary\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"typescript\": \"^5.3.0\",\n    \"tsx\": \"^4.7.0\",\n    \"vitest\": \"^1.0.0\",\n    \"@vitest/coverage-v8\": \"^1.0.0\",\n    \"eslint\": \"^9.0.0\",\n    \"eslint-plugin-sonarjs\": \"^1.0.0\",\n    \"typescript-eslint\": \"^8.0.0\"\n  },\n  \"pnpm\": {\n    \"onlyBuiltDependencies\": [\"esbuild\"]\n  }\n}\n---\n{\n  \"compilerOptions\": {\n    \"target\": \"ES2022\",\n    \"module\": \"ESNext\",\n    \"moduleResolution\": \"Node\",\n    \"strict\": true,\n    \"esModuleInterop\": true,\n    \"skipLibCheck\": true,\n    \"outDir\": \"./dist\"\n  },\n  \"include\": [\"src/**/*\"]\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951300380},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_c629a84540ad4a7885e16cef","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"ls -la src/ && echo \"---SKILLS---\" && ls -la .pi/skills/ && echo \"---AGENTS---\" && ls -la .pi/agents/ 2>/dev/null && echo \"---\" && cat vitest.config.ts && echo \"---\" && cat metrics.json"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":697,"output":70,"cacheRead":7936,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":8703,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951300381,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-4fd93f0d-e4ae-4f1f-b884-cabc7dff0ef7","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_c629a84540ad4a7885e16cef","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"total 8\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 ..\n---SKILLS---\ntotal 20\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 green\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 red\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 test-list\n---AGENTS---\ntotal 16\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 03:48 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 experimenter experimenter 7841 Jul 25 03:48 refactor.md\n---\nimport { defineConfig } from 'vitest/config';\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  test: {\n    include: ['src/**/*.spec.ts'],\n    globals: true,\n    coverage: {\n      provider: 'v8',\n      reporter: ['json-summary', 'text'],\n      include: ['src/**/*.ts'],\n      exclude: ['src/**/*.spec.ts'],\n    },\n  },\n});\n---\n{\n  \"kata\": \"claim-office-example-mapping\",\n  \"workflow\": \"v6.2-with-why-cleaned-pi\",\n  \"model\": \"glm-5-2\",\n  \"cli_model\": \"pi-only\",\n  \"thinking\": false,\n  \"started_at\": \"2026-07-25T03:48:17+00:00\",\n  \"ended_at\": null,\n  \"duration_seconds\": null,\n  \"batch_run\": true,\n  \"plan_file\": \"/home/experimenter/experiments/batch-plans/rq-model-novel-pi-fill.shard5.json\"\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951301043},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_444ce917cfca49b6862ac1b1","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":563,"output":19,"cacheRead":8640,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":9222,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951301044,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-142e5646-7be7-45d3-a84f-16ee63c1bd68","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_444ce917cfca49b6862ac1b1","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: test-list\ndescription: TDD Test List Phase - Create a comprehensive test list covering every example and rule from the specification\n---\n\n# TDD Test List Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Test List Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to create a comprehensive test list.\n\n## Your Mission\n\nCreate a test list using `it.todo()` that covers **every rule and every example** from the specification:\n1. Read the specification (`prompt.md`) thoroughly -- every rule, every example, every clarifying question (?)\n2. Turn each example into at least one `it.todo()` test case\n3. Order tests from simplest to most complex\n4. Use `it.todo()` only -- NO executable tests yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Understand the Feature\nRead the complete specification. Pay special attention to integration examples and clarifying\nquestions (marked with ?) -- these disambiguate rules that may seem open to interpretation in isolation.\n- What are all the operations the system must support?\n- What rules govern each operation?\n- Which examples in the spec illustrate these rules?\n\n### Step 2: Identify Test Cases from the Spec\nWalk through the specification section by section. For each rule and each example:\n- Create a test case that verifies the described behavior\n- Include the **expected values from the spec** in the test description\n- If a clarifying question (?) resolves an ambiguity, create a test for the clarified interpretation\n- If the spec uses an example-mapping format (rules, examples, questions), every listed example must have a corresponding test\n\n### Step 3: Order Tests (Simple -> Complex)\nArrange tests in increasing complexity:\n1. Simplest case (often empty/zero/single item)\n2. Individual rules in isolation\n3. Rules with modifiers\n4. Combinations of multiple rules\n5. Multi-step scenarios (e.g., operations that reference earlier results)\n\n### Step 4: Write Test File\nCreate the test file with `it.todo()` entries:\n\n```typescript\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { functionName } from \"./implementation.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Feature Name\", () => {\n  it.todo(\"should [behavior] -- [expected value from spec]\");\n  it.todo(\"should [next behavior] -- [expected value from spec]\");\n  // ... ordered simple -> complex, covering ALL spec examples\n});\n```\n\n### Step 5: Provide Summary\n\nAfter creating the test list, output:\n\n```\nTest List Created:\n**Feature**: [feature name]\n**Test File**: [filename].spec.ts\n**Tests**: [count]\n\n**Test Cases** (ordered simple -> complex):\n1. [first test description]\n2. [second test description]\n3. [third test description]\n...\n\n**Next Step**: Invoke `red` skill to activate the first test.\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Cover **every spec example** with at least one test\n- Cover **every operation** described in the spec\n- Give **every clarifying question (?)** a corresponding test\n- Order tests **simple -> complex**\n- Use `it.todo()` for all tests\n- Include **expected values** in descriptions\n- Keep tests **independent**\n- One behavior per test\n\n### DON'T\n- Write executable tests (use `it.todo()`)\n- Think about implementation instead of behavior\n- Miss an entire operation described in the spec\n- Order randomly\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing the test list, proceed to Red phase:\n\n```\nTest List Phase Complete. Proceeding to Red phase with the first test.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951301523},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_d2aa840ad4354fb8b3367a0e","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/red/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":757,"output":18,"cacheRead":9216,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":9991,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951301524,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-61657266-4aff-4c8f-b6a7-174933b6e258","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_d2aa840ad4354fb8b3367a0e","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: red\ndescription: TDD Red Phase - Activate ONE test from the test list and make it fail with explicit predictions\n---\n\n# TDD Red Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Red Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to activate ONE test and make it fail.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Activate exactly ONE test from the test list\n2. Make explicit predictions about how it will fail\n3. Verify the test fails for the right reason\n4. Maintain strict discipline - NO implementation during Red phase\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Red Phase Rules\n\n- **One test at a time**: Convert exactly ONE `it.todo()` to executable test\n- **All other tests remain as `it.todo()`**: Never have more than one failing test\n- **Two-stage failure**: First compilation error, then runtime/assertion error\n- **Make predictions**: Explicitly state expected failures before running tests\n- **No implementation**: Don't write code to make test pass yet\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Activate One Test\n\nIdentify the next `it.todo()` and convert it to executable test code:\n\n```typescript\n// Convert from:\nit.todo(\"should return 0 for empty input\");\n\n// To:\nit(\"should return 0 for empty input\", () => {\n  expect(calculate(\"\")).toBe(0);\n});\n```\n\nLeave all other tests as `it.todo()`.\n\n### Step 2: Predict Compilation Error\n\nBefore running the test, state your prediction:\n\n```\nRed Phase - Compilation Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n- Expected: Compilation error\n- Reason: Function `calculate` doesn't exist yet\n- Error: \"Cannot find name 'calculate'\"\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Test - Verify Compilation Error\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Compilation error as predicted, OR\n- Prediction wrong -> STOP and explain discrepancy\n\n### Step 4: Create Empty Function\n\nCreate minimal function stub (no logic):\n\n```typescript\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return undefined as unknown as number; // Intentionally wrong\n};\n```\n\n### Step 5: Predict Runtime Error\n\nBefore running again, state your prediction:\n\n```\nRed Phase - Runtime Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n- Expected: Runtime assertion error\n- Expected value: 0\n- Actual value: undefined\n- Diff:\n  Expected: 0\n  Received: undefined\n```\n\n### Step 6: Run Test - Verify Runtime Error\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Assertion error as predicted, OR\n- Prediction wrong -> STOP and explain discrepancy\n\n### Step 7: Report Completion\n\nYou MUST output the full Step 7 block verbatim with `Correct` or `Incorrect`\nchosen for each prediction. Do not abbreviate. Do not collapse the two\nprediction lines into one.\n\n**Why this format matters:** The block is mechanically parsed to compute\n`predictions_correct_rate`. The parser expects two lines matching\n`(- |Correct|Incorrect)` per cycle -- one for the compilation prediction,\none for the runtime prediction. Collapsing them into a single line, summarizing\nthem as \"both correct\", or skipping the block entirely drops the predictions\ncount for this cycle to zero. Format consistency here directly drives a metric\nthe experiment measures.\n\n```\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Test Activated**: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n**Compilation Prediction**: Cannot find name 'calculate' Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected 0, received undefined Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase.\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Activate exactly ONE test at a time\n- Make explicit predictions before running tests\n- Verify test fails for the right reason\n- Keep all other tests as `it.todo()`\n\n### DON'T\n- Activate multiple tests\n- Skip making predictions\n- Write implementation to make test pass\n- Continue if prediction fails without explanation\n\n## Prediction Failure Protocol\n\nIf your prediction was wrong:\n\n```\nPrediction Failed:\n- Predicted: [what you expected]\n- Actual: [what happened]\n- Discrepancy: [explanation]\n\nInvestigating the discrepancy before proceeding.\n```\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Red phase, proceed to Green phase:\n\n```\nRed Phase Complete. Proceeding to Green phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951302017},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_f65c730e62b641b49ea3f469","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/green/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":927,"output":19,"cacheRead":9984,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":10930,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951302018,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-f068f128-1b0e-4b33-ae7c-80d567d3fd3b","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_f65c730e62b641b49ea3f469","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: green\ndescription: TDD Green Phase - Implement minimal code to make the failing test pass\n---\n\n# TDD Green Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Green Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to make the failing test pass with MINIMAL code.\n\n## Why minimality matters\n\nThe Green Phase deliberately writes the smallest implementation that turns the active test green -- even hardcoded returns, even obviously-incomplete logic. This is not laziness or naivete:\n\n- It **exposes refactoring opportunities** in the next phase. When the implementation does exactly one thing, structural smells are visible. Over-implemented green code looks \"already clean\" and hides design problems.\n- It **prevents premature generalization**. The shape of the right abstraction emerges from the second and third test, not the first. Implementing for hypothetical future tests locks in a design before its constraints are known.\n- It **keeps the red-green-refactor cycle short**. Each cycle pays a cost in tokens and context; over-implementing in green collapses several future cycles into one large one and loses the per-test feedback loop.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Implement the **minimal code** necessary to make the failing test pass\n2. Use the **simplest possible solution** (hardcoded values are acceptable)\n3. Avoid adding features for future tests\n4. Verify all tests pass\n5. NO optimization, NO refactoring yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Green Phase Rules\n\n- **Minimal code only**: Just enough to pass the current test\n- **Baby steps**: Make the smallest possible change\n- **No future features**: Don't implement what future tests might need\n- **Simple is better**: Hardcoded returns are perfectly fine\n- **Tests must pass**: Verify all tests are green\n- **No refactoring yet**: Save improvements for Refactor phase\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Failing Test\n\nUnderstand what the test expects:\n- What input does the test provide?\n- What output does it expect?\n- What is the **simplest** way to produce that output?\n\n### Step 2: Write Minimal Implementation\n\nImplement **only what's needed** to make the current test pass:\n\n```typescript\n// For first test \"should return 0 for empty input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return 0; // Minimal - just make the test pass\n};\n\n// For second test \"should return number for single input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  return parseInt(input); // Still simple\n};\n\n// For third test \"should add two numbers\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  const numbers = input.split(\",\");\n  if (numbers.length === 1) return parseInt(numbers[0]);\n  return parseInt(numbers[0]) + parseInt(numbers[1]); // Only now add logic\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Tests\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Current test now passes\n- All previous tests still pass\n\n### Step 4: Verify No Over-Implementation\n\nCheck yourself:\n- Did I implement features for future tests? -> Remove them\n- Did I add logic not demanded by current test? -> Remove it\n- Did I optimize prematurely? -> Simplify\n- Did I refactor existing code? -> Revert, save for Refactor phase\n\n### Step 5: Report Completion\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete:\n**Implementation**: [brief description of what was added]\n**Result**: All tests now pass ([X] passing)\n**Approach**: [explain why this is minimal]\n\nProceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n\n## Minimal Implementation Strategies\n\n### Hardcoded Returns (Preferred for Early Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\nreturn 0; // Perfect - minimal\n```\n\n### Simple Conditionals (When Multiple Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return number for single input\"\nif (input === \"\") return 0;\nreturn parseInt(input); // Still simple\n```\n\n### Generalization (Only When Forced)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should add multiple numbers\" - NOW generalize\nreturn input.split(\",\").reduce((sum, n) => sum + parseInt(n), 0);\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Write minimal code to make test pass\n- Use hardcoded values when appropriate\n- Take baby steps\n- Verify all tests pass\n\n### DON'T\n- Implement beyond what tests demand\n- Add features for future tests\n- Optimize prematurely\n- Refactor during Green phase\n\n## Psychological Resistance\n\nYou will feel resistance:\n- **\"This is too simple\"** -> That's correct! Minimal is the way\n- **\"Hardcoded values feel wrong\"** -> They're exactly right for early tests\n- **\"I should implement ahead\"** -> Resist this strongly\n- **\"This is inefficient\"** -> Actually accelerates development\n\nTrust the process. Simple steps compound into elegant solutions.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Green phase, proceed to Refactor phase:\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete. Proceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951302661},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_1fd288e48f43423b9a9e753f","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/agents/refactor.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":1139,"output":16,"cacheRead":10880,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":12035,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784951302661,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-fba0638f-7710-4a8d-88b1-d73c369d0fba","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_1fd288e48f43423b9a9e753f","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: refactor\ndescription: Refactoring specialist. Applies Simple Design Rules and the Absolute Priority Premise (APP) to improve code while keeping all tests green. Returns a summary of what changed and why.\ntools: read, write, edit, bash, grep, find, ls\n---\n\n\n## Your Mission\n\nGuide the requester through a refactoring pass by helping them:\n1. **MUST attempt at least one refactoring** - mandatory, not optional\n2. Apply the Four Rules of Simple Design in priority order\n3. Use Absolute Priority Premise (APP) to measure code improvements\n4. Improve code quality while keeping all tests green\n5. Document refactoring decisions and mass calculations\n6. If no improvement is possible, explicitly document why\n\n## Refactoring Rules\n\n- **Mandatory refactoring attempt**: MUST try at least one improvement\n- **Tests must stay green**: Never break passing tests\n- **Apply Simple Design Rules**: In priority order (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4)\n- **Calculate APP mass**: Before and after refactoring\n- **Document decisions**: Explain improvements or why none were possible\n- **Naming is first priority**: Evaluate if function name still fits its purpose\n\n### Simple Design Rules (Priority Order)\n\n#### Rule 1: Tests Pass\n- **Highest priority** - never compromise working code\n- All tests must pass before and after refactoring\n- If tests fail, revert and try different approach\n\n#### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n- **Second priority** - clarity trumps everything else (including APP)\n- Use meaningful names for variables, functions, classes\n- Structure code to be self-documenting\n- Prefer explicit over clever code\n- **Naming Evaluation (First Refactoring Priority)**:\n  - Ask: \"Does this name clearly describe what the function actually does based on all tests we have so far?\"\n  - Ask: \"Has the function's purpose become clearer/more specific through the latest test?\"\n  - Rename if the name doesn't capture the current full intent\n  - Especially critical when new functionality changes the nature of what the function does\n\n#### Rule 3: No Duplication (DRY)\n- **Third priority** - extract common functionality\n- Look for obvious and conceptual duplication\n- Knowledge should have single representation\n- **Balance with Rule 2**: If DRY hurts clarity, choose clarity\n\n#### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- **Lowest priority** - minimize code elements\n- Remove unnecessary abstractions\n- Keep it simple - don't over-engineer\n- Only add complexity when it serves clear purpose\n\n### Absolute Priority Premise (APP)\n\n#### Mass Calculation\n```\nTotal Mass = (constants x 1) + (bindings x 1) + (invocations x 2) +\n             (conditionals x 4) + (loops x 5) + (assignments x 6)\n```\n\n#### Component Values\n- **Constant** (Mass: 1): Literal values (`5`, `\"hello\"`, `true`)\n- **Binding/Scalar** (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters (`amount`, `result`)\n- **Invocation** (Mass: 2): Function calls (`calculate()`, `Math.max()`)\n- **Conditional** (Mass: 4): Control flow (`if`, `switch`, `?:`)\n- **Loop** (Mass: 5): Iteration (`for`, `forEach`, `map`)\n- **Assignment** (Mass: 6): Mutations (`x = 5`, `count++`)\n\n#### Guidelines\n- **Lower mass = Better code** (generally)\n- **Rule 2 trumps APP**: Clarity over low mass\n- **Use during refactoring**: Compare before/after mass\n- **Context matters**: Don't sacrifice readability for mass\n\n## Refactoring Process\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\nBefore anything else, evaluate the naming:\n```\n**Naming Evaluation**:\n- Current name: `calculate`\n- Function purpose: \"adds numbers from an array\"\n- Question: Does \"calculate\" clearly reveal this intent?\n- Assessment: Too generic - \"calculate\" could mean anything\n- Recommendation: Rename to `sumNumbers` or keep if name fits\n\nDecision: [Rename to X] or [Keep current name because Y]\n```\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Initial APP Mass\nBefore making changes, calculate current code mass:\n```\n**Current Code Mass**:\nfunction calculate(numbers: number[]): number {\n  return numbers.reduce((sum, num) => sum + num, 0);\n}\n\nComponent Count:\n- Constants: 1 (literal 0) = 1\n- Bindings: 3 (numbers, sum, num) = 3\n- Invocations: 2 (reduce, +) = 4\n- Conditionals: 0 = 0\n- Loops: 1 (reduce is iteration) = 5\n- Assignments: 0 = 0\n\nTotal Mass: 13\n```\n\n### Step 3: Apply Simple Design Rules (in order)\nSystematically evaluate each rule:\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 1: Tests Pass\n- Are all tests currently passing?\n- If not, fix before refactoring\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n- Are names clear and descriptive?\n- Is code structure self-documenting?\n- Can intent be improved?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Rename variables for clarity\n- Extract explaining variables\n- Rename functions to match purpose\n- Restructure for readability\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 3: No Duplication\n- Is there duplicated code?\n- Is there conceptual duplication?\n- Can common logic be extracted?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Extract helper functions\n- Remove copy-paste code\n- Consolidate similar logic\n\n#### Evaluate Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n- Are there unnecessary abstractions?\n- Can code be simplified?\n- Are all elements necessary?\n\nPotential improvements:\n- Remove unused functions/variables\n- Simplify over-engineered solutions\n- Inline unnecessary extractions\n\n### Step 4: Implement Refactoring\n- Make ONE improvement at a time\n- Run tests after each change\n- Ensure tests stay green\n- If tests fail, revert change\n\n### Step 5: Calculate New APP Mass\nAfter refactoring, recalculate mass:\n```\n**Refactored Code Mass**:\n[refactored code]\n\nComponent Count:\n[detailed breakdown]\n\nTotal Mass: [new total]\nMass Change: [old mass] -> [new mass] (delta [difference])\n```\n\n### Step 6: Document Decision\nExplain the refactoring outcome:\n\n**If Improvements Made:**\n```\n**Refactoring Applied**:\n- Naming: Renamed `calculate` to `sumNumbers` (better reveals intent)\n- Mass: Reduced from 13 to 11 (removed conditional)\n- Rule 2: Improved clarity with explaining variable\n\nBenefits:\n- Code now clearly states it sums numbers\n- Reduced complexity (lower mass)\n- More maintainable\n```\n\n**If No Improvements Possible:**\n```\n**Refactoring Evaluation**:\n- Naming: `calculate` already clearly describes purpose\n- Duplication: No duplicated code found\n- Mass: Current implementation already minimal (mass: 13)\n- Simplification: No unnecessary complexity\n\nReasoning:\nCurrent implementation is already optimal because:\n1. Name clearly reveals intent\n2. No duplication exists\n3. Mass is minimal for this functionality\n4. No unnecessary abstractions\n\nNo refactoring performed - code is already clean.\n```\n\n### Step 7: Report Completion\n```\nRefactoring Complete:\n**Refactoring**: [improvements made or \"none possible\"]\n**Mass Change**: [before -> after] (if calculated)\n**Tests**: All passing\n```\n\nReturn the report to the requester.\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### What to DO\n- MUST attempt at least one refactoring\n- Evaluate naming FIRST\n- Calculate APP mass before and after\n- Apply Simple Design Rules in priority order\n- Keep tests green at all times\n- Document all decisions\n- Explain why if no improvement possible\n\n### What NOT to do\n- Never return without attempting at least one improvement\n- Never break tests during refactoring\n- Never sacrifice clarity for lower mass\n- Never refactor multiple things at once\n- Never say \"no refactoring needed\" without detailed explanation\n\n## Remember\n\n- **Mandatory refactoring attempt** - MUST try at least one improvement\n- **Naming first** - Always evaluate function names first\n- **Tests stay green** - Never break passing tests\n- **Simple Design Rules** - Apply in priority order (1 -> 2 -> 3 -> 4)\n- **Rule 2 trumps APP** - Clarity over low mass\n- **Document everything** - Mass calculations and decisions\n\nYour goal is to systematically improve code quality using established principles, measure improvements objectively with APP, and maintain transparency through comprehensive documentation.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784951303198},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the workflow. Let me check the run.log to understand if anything was already attempted, but actually, the instructions say to start fresh. Let me look at the eslint config too.\n\nLet me understand the kata requirements fully. This is a complex insurance system. Let me break it down:\n\n**Feature: MHPCO Claim Office**\n- Two operations: `quote` and `claim`\n- CLI reads JSON from stdin, writes JSON to stdout\n\n**Quote** computes premium for a list of items.\n**Claim** processes a damage report against an existing policy.\n\nLet me carefully enumerate all the rules and examples to build a comprehensive test list.\n\n### Item values and base premiums\n- Sword: 1000 G insurance value, 100 G base premium\n- Amulet: 600 G / 60 G\n- Staff: 800 G / 80 G\n- Potion: 400 G / 40 G\n- Components (runes, moonstones): 250 G each, 25 G base premium per component\n- Building block of 3 alike components: special base premium of 60 G\n\n### Premium modifiers\n- Cursed items: +50% risk surcharge (item-specific)\n- Highly enchanted (enchantment ≥ 5): +30% risk surcharge (item-specific)\n- Long-standing customers (≥ 2 years): 20% loyalty discount (policy-wide)\n- First insurance: 10% initial assessment surcharge (policy-wide? Need to check)\n- Customers receive 15% discount on each contract after their first (policy-wide)\n- 5 G processing fee added to every premium\n\nAll amounts rounded to whole G in MHPCO's favor.\n\nWait, let me re-read the modifier scope:\n- item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the base premium of the affected item\n- policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium (the sum of all item base premiums)\n- the processing fee is added at the very end\n\nSo the first insurance surcharge and follow-up contract discount are policy-wide.\n\nFrom the integration examples:\n- Newcomer with a cursed sword: 0 years, no previous contract, cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3) → premium 165 G\n  - 100 base + 50 curse + 10 first insurance = 160 + 5 fee = 165\n  - Wait, \"first insurance\" is 10 G. But it says \"10 % initial assessment surcharge\". 10% of 100 = 10. And the curse is 50% of 100 = 50. So item-specific surcharges are percentages of item base premium, but they're added as flat amounts (50 G, 10 G).\n  - Actually wait, first insurance is policy-wide (10% of policy base premium). Policy base premium = 100. 10% = 10. OK.\n  - So the policy base premium here = 100 (just the sword). Curse adds 50 (50% of sword's base). High enchantment: none (enchantment 3). First insurance: 10 (10% of 100). \n  - Total before fee: 100 + 50 + 10 = 160. Plus 5 fee = 165. ✓\n\n- Long-standing customer's second contract: 3 years, second quote, cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7) → premium 160 G\n  - 100 base + 50 curse + 30 high enchantment − 20 loyalty + 10 first insurance − 15 follow-up contract = 155 + 5 fee = 160\n  - Policy base = 100. Curse = 50. High enchantment = 30 (30% of 100). \n  - Loyalty = -20 (20% of 100). First insurance = +10 (10% of 100). Follow-up = -15 (15% of 100).\n  - 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155. + 5 = 160. ✓\n  - Note: \"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n  - So \"first insurance\" means the customer's first time insuring that particular item (each quote is a \"first insurance\" for the items in it). Actually the clarification says \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\" So first insurance surcharge ALWAYS applies to every quote.\n  \n  Hmm, but wait. Then when does the \"follow-up contract\" discount apply? \"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\" So the follow-up discount applies when this is NOT the customer's first contract. The \"first insurance\" surcharge always applies (each item treated as first insurance). \n  \n  But how do we know if it's the customer's first contract or a follow-up? From the scenario: \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\". So we count the number of quote steps that have occurred so far in the scenario for this customer. If it's >= 2nd quote, follow-up discount applies. First insurance always applies.\n\nWait, let me reconsider. Actually let me re-read:\n\n\"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\"\n\"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\"\n\nAnd from integration:\n\"The first insurance surcharge still applies to the new sword, even though the customer is on a follow-up contract — each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nSo:\n- First insurance surcharge: applies to EVERY quote (each item treated as first insurance). 10% of policy base premium.\n- Follow-up contract discount: 15% of policy base premium, applies when this is NOT the customer's first quote in the scenario.\n\nHmm, but \"first contract\" — first ever, or first in scenario? The integration example says \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\". So it seems like we track within the scenario. Let me think... Actually, the customer object only has `yearsWithMHPCO`. There's no \"previous contracts\" field. So the only way to know if it's a follow-up is by counting quotes in the scenario. \n\nSo: follow-up discount applies when the quote step is NOT the first quote step in the scenario (i.e., it's the 2nd, 3rd, etc. quote step). First insurance surcharge always applies.\n\nActually wait — let me reconsider. Maybe the first insurance surcharge applies only on the first quote (first contract), and follow-up discount applies on subsequent. But the integration example explicitly says the first insurance surcharge applies even on the second contract (follow-up). So:\n- First insurance: ALWAYS (every quote).\n- Follow-up: on 2nd+ quote in scenario.\n\nHmm, that's a bit odd but that's what the spec says. Let me re-read once more:\n\n\"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\"\n\nYes. So first insurance always applies. Follow-up applies on contracts after the first (i.e., 2nd+ quote step in scenario).\n\nOK so let me reconsider the newcomer example: 0 years, no previous contract (first quote), cursed sword (steel, enchantment 3):\n- base = 100\n- curse = +50 (50% of 100)\n- high enchant = 0 (enchant 3 < 5)\n- loyalty = 0 (0 years < 2)\n- first insurance = +10 (10% of 100)\n- follow-up = 0 (first quote, no follow-up discount)\n- total before fee = 100 + 50 + 10 = 160\n- + 5 fee = 165 ✓\n\nLong-standing second contract: 3 years, 2nd quote, cursed sword (steel, enchantment 7):\n- base = 100\n- curse = +50\n- high enchant = +30 (enchant 7 ≥ 5)\n- loyalty = -20 (3 years ≥ 2)\n- first insurance = +10\n- follow-up = -15 (2nd quote)\n- total before fee = 100 + 50 + 30 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155\n- + 5 fee = 160 ✓\n\nGreat, my understanding is correct.\n\n### Building block of 3 alike components\n- 2 runes → 50 G base premium (2 × 25)\n- 3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies: 60 instead of 75)\n- 4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3; 4 × 25)\n- 7 runes → 175 G base premium (7 × 25)\n\nWait, 7 runes = 175 = 7 × 25. So no block for 7? Block only applies for exactly 3 alike. So if you have 6 runes, that's 6 × 25 = 150? Or 2 blocks = 120? The spec says \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" And \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\". So blocks can stack: 6 runes = 2 blocks = 120 G.\n\nBut 7 runes = 175 = 7 × 25 = 175. So 7 runes is NOT 2 blocks + 1 = 60+60+25 = 145. Hmm. So the block only applies to EXACTLY 3, and you can have multiple blocks of exactly 3.\n\nWait, but 7 = 3 + 3 + 1 = 2 blocks + 1 rune = 120 + 25 = 145. But spec says 175. So that doesn't work either.\n\nLet me re-read: \"3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\". \"4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\". \"7 runes → 175 G base premium\".\n\nSo 4 runes = 100 = 4 × 25. No block (block requires exactly 3). 7 runes = 175 = 7 × 25. No block.\n\nHmm so when does a block apply? Only when the count is exactly 3? But \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\" = 60 + 60 = 120. So 3 of one type = 1 block, 3 of another type = another block.\n\nBut what about 6 runes? The spec doesn't directly say. Let me think. 6 runes = 2 × 3 = 2 blocks = 120? Or 6 × 25 = 150? \n\nThe examples: 2 runes = 50, 3 runes = 60 (block), 4 runes = 100 (no block), 7 runes = 175 (no block).\n\nSo it seems like the block ONLY applies when you have EXACTLY 3 of a type. If you have 4, no block. If you have 7, no block. So 6 runes... hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe the block applies per-group-of-exactly-3. Like, you partition the components into blocks of 3 where possible, but only if the total count for that type is a multiple of 3? No...\n\nActually, \"block requires exactly 3\" — for 4 runes, the comment is \"no block — block requires exactly 3\". So if you have 4 runes, you can't form a block (because 4 is not exactly 3). For 7 runes, same. So the block only applies when the count of a type is EXACTLY 3.\n\nBut then 6 runes: count is 6, not exactly 3, so no block → 6 × 25 = 150.\n\nHmm, but that seems weird (6 runes costs more than... well, 3 runes = 60, 6 runes would be 150, but 2× \"3 runes\" = 120). But the spec only gives examples for 2, 3, 4, 7. Let me just go with: block applies when count of a type is exactly 3. Actually, let me reconsider — maybe the intent is that you can only form a block of exactly 3, and leftover components are charged individually. So:\n- 3 runes: 1 block = 60.\n- 4 runes: 1 block + 1 = 60 + 25 = 85? But spec says 100. So NO. \n\nSo 4 runes is NOT 1 block + 1. It's 4 × 25 = 100. So the block does NOT \"break off\" 3 and leave the rest. The block only applies when ALL components of that type number exactly 3.\n\nHmm OK. So the rule is: for each component type, if the count is exactly 3, charge 60 (block). Otherwise charge count × 25.\n\nWait, but what about \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → 120 G base premium (two separate blocks)\". 3 runes = 60, 3 moonstones = 60, total = 120. ✓. And \"2 runes + 1 moonstone → 75 G base premium (no block: different types)\". 2 runes = 50, 1 moonstone = 25, total = 75. ✓ (no block because neither type has exactly 3).\n\nSo: for each type, if count == 3, base = 60; else base = count × 25.\n\nBut what about 6 of a type? Not in examples. I'll assume: if count == 3, block (60). The spec literally says \"block requires exactly 3\" and \"no block — block requires exactly 3\" for 4. So I think the rule is exactly count === 3 → block. For 6, no block (150). Hmm, but that's a weird business rule. Let me re-read the original rule statement:\n\n\"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\"\n\nAnd examples say \"block requires exactly 3\". So I'll go with: a block is exactly 3 alike components → 60 G. If a type has exactly 3, it's a block (60). Otherwise per-component (25 each). I'll test the given examples (2, 3, 4, 7, and the mixed ones). I won't add a test for 6 unless... actually the examples don't cover 6, so I won't test 6. Good — that avoids ambiguity.\n\nActually wait, let me reconsider whether maybe blocks are formed greedily. Let me check: is there any reading where 7 runes = 175 makes sense with blocks? 7 = 2×3 + 1 = 120 + 25 = 145 ≠ 175. 7 = 1 block + 4 = 60 + 100 = 160 ≠ 175. No. 7 = 7 × 25 = 175. So no blocks for 7. And the comment explicitly says nothing about a block for 7. The example for 4 says \"(no block — block requires exactly 3)\". So the rule is clearly: block applies ONLY when count is exactly 3.\n\nOK so my rule: for each component type, if count === 3, premium = 60; else premium = count × 25.\n\nHmm wait, but what about count = 6? With my rule, 6 → 150 (no block). But maybe it should be 2 blocks = 120? The spec is ambiguous, but since there's no example for 6, I won't test it. Actually, let me reconsider once more. The phrase \"block requires exactly 3\" most naturally means: to form a block you need exactly 3 alike. It doesn't say \"exactly 3 total\". But the 4-rune example (100 = 4×25, no block) shows that having 4 does NOT give you 1 block + 1 extra. If blocks formed greedily, 4 = block(60) + 1(25) = 85, not 100. So blocks do NOT form greedily; you only get a block if the entire group is exactly 3.\n\nSo 6 runes: the group is 6, not exactly 3, so no block → 150. I'm confident. But I won't test it.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider 6 one more time, because \"3 runes + 3 moonstones → two separate blocks (120)\" shows blocks can be combined across types. So if you have 6 runes, could that be \"two blocks of 3 runes\"? The group is 6. Hmm. If the rule were \"count must be a multiple of 3\", then 6 → 2 blocks = 120, and 3 → 1 block = 60, and 9 → 3 blocks = 180. But 4 and 7 are not multiples of 3, so no block. That's consistent with the examples too!\n\nSo there are two consistent interpretations:\n(a) block applies iff count === 3 exactly.\n(b) block applies iff count is a multiple of 3 (count % 3 === 0), with count/3 blocks.\n\nBoth give the same answer for 2, 3, 4, 7. They differ for 6, 9, etc. Since there's no example for 6, I can't disambiguate from the spec. I'll choose NOT to add a test for 6 (since it's ambiguous), and implement... hmm, but I have to implement something. Let me think about which is more likely intended.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"A building block of 3 alike components is offered at a special base premium of 60 G.\" This suggests a block IS a group of 3. \"3 runes → 60 G base premium (block applies)\". \"4 runes → 100 G base premium (no block — block requires exactly 3)\". \n\nHmm, \"block requires exactly 3\" — I think this means a block consists of exactly 3. If you have 4, you have 1 block + 1 leftover, but the leftover... wait but then 4 = 60 + 25 = 85, not 100. Unless leftover can't be combined and... no, 85 ≠ 100.\n\nSo the ONLY way 4 runes = 100 is if NO block is formed at all (4 × 25 = 100). That means the block is all-or-nothing per type: either the whole group is a block (count === 3) or it's all individual (count × 25).\n\nBut then 6 runes = 150 (all individual). And interpretation (b) (multiple of 3) would give 120. These differ.\n\nI think the most literal reading of \"block requires exactly 3\" is interpretation (a): the block is formed only when the count is exactly 3. So 6 runes = 150.\n\nBut honestly, the business intent might be (b). Since the spec is ambiguous and there's no example, I'll go with (a) — count === 3 → block — because it's the most literal reading of \"block requires exactly 3\", and the 4-rune example reinforces \"exactly\" (not \"at least 3\" or \"multiple of 3\").\n\nHmm, actually, wait. Let me reconsider. There's a subtle point. What if blocks form greedily but only complete groups of 3 form a block, and the remainder is individual? Then:\n- 3 → 1 block = 60 ✓\n- 4 → 1 block + 1 = 60 + 25 = 85 ✗ (spec says 100)\n\nSo that's ruled out. Good.\n\nWhat if the rule is: a block forms only if ALL components of that type can form complete blocks (count % 3 === 0)? Then:\n- 3 → 1 block = 60 ✓\n- 4 → 4 % 3 ≠ 0 → no block → 100 ✓\n- 7 → 7 % 3 ≠ 0 → no block → 175 ✓\n- 6 → 6 % 3 === 0 → 2 blocks = 120\n\nThis is interpretation (b). It's consistent with all examples.\n\nAnd interpretation (a):\n- 3 → block = 60 ✓\n- 4 → no block → 100 ✓\n- 7 → no block → 175 ✓\n- 6 → no block → 150\n\nBoth consistent. Since I won't test 6, either works for passing tests. I'll implement (a) for simplicity (count === 3 → 60 else count × 25), but actually, hmm, let me think about which is \"more correct\" in case the hidden grader checks. The grader presumably uses the same prompt. But the prompt doesn't specify 6. So the grader's reference solution could be either. Risky. But I have no way to know. I'll go with (a) since it's the most literal.\n\nHmm, actually, you know what, let me reconsider. \"block requires exactly 3\" — I now think this is saying: to GET the block price, you need exactly 3 (not 4, not 7). It's emphasizing the count must be 3. So (a). \n\nOK moving on. Let me not overthink; I'll only test the given examples.\n\n### Modifier scope on multi-item policies\n- policy covers cursed sword (base 100) + plain amulet (base 60) → policy base 160; cursed surcharge adds 50 (50% of cursed sword's base 100) → 210 before further modifiers and fee.\n- So item-specific modifiers apply to that item's base. Policy-wide to the sum.\n\n### Modifier thresholds\n- customer with exactly 2 years → loyalty discount applies (so ≥ 2, inclusive).\n- sword with exactly enchantment 5 → high-enchantment surcharge applies; if cursed, both apply.\n- sword with enchantment 4 → no high-enchantment surcharge; curse surcharge applies only if cursed.\n- dragon-material sword with exactly enchantment 8, damage 1000 G → payout 400 G (high-enchantment clause applies, then deductible). \n\nWait: enchantment 8 ≥ 8, so high-enchantment claim clause (50% reimbursement). Damage 1000 → 50% = 500 → minus deductible 100 = 400. ✓. (This is the CLAIM clause for enchantment ≥ 8, which is different from the premium modifier for enchantment ≥ 5.)\n\nNote: there are TWO enchantment thresholds:\n- Premium: enchantment ≥ 5 → +30% surcharge.\n- Claim: enchantment ≥ 8 → 50% reimbursement.\n\n### Deductible per damage event\n- dragon attack damages sword (500) and amulet (300); payout = 600 (100 deductible per damaged item). 500 - 100 + 300 - 100 = 400 + 200 = 600. ✓\n\n### Standard reimbursement (no special clauses)\n- regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 → payout 400 (full minus 100 deductible).\n- damage to a rune (value 250), damage 200 → payout 100 (full minus 100 deductible; runes have no enchantment/material).\n\n### Enchantment threshold vs. dragon material\n- dragon-material sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 → payout 400 (both clauses apply; 50% rule wins, then deductible: 500 - 100).\n  - Wait, \"both clauses apply; the 50% rule wins\". So both the high-enchantment (≥8) 50% clause AND the dragon-material full-reimbursement clause apply. The 50% rule \"wins\" → 500, then deductible → 400.\n  - Hmm, so when both apply, the 50% rule wins (i.e., 50% reimbursement, not full). So dragon material gives full reimbursement, but high enchantment (≥8) overrides to 50%.\n- dragon-material sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 → payout 700 (only dragon-material clause applies: full reimbursement, then deductible: 800 - 100). Enchantment 5 < 8, so no 50% clause. Dragon material → full. 800 - 100 = 700. ✓\n- steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 → payout 400 (only high-enchantment clause: 50% first, then deductible: 500 - 100). Steel (not dragon) → no full reimbursement. Enchantment 9 ≥ 8 → 50%. 500 - 100 = 400. ✓\n\nSo claim reimbursement logic per damage entry:\n1. Determine reimbursement rate:\n   - If item has enchantment ≥ 8: 50% (high-enchantment clause, overrides dragon).\n   - Else if item is dragon material: 100% (full).\n   - Else: 100% (full, standard).\n   \n   Wait, but \"both clauses apply; the 50% rule wins\" for dragon + enchantment ≥ 8. And \"only the dragon-material clause applies\" for dragon + enchantment 5 (full). And \"only the high-enchantment clause applies\" for steel + enchantment 9 (50%).\n   \n   So: if enchantment ≥ 8 → 50% (regardless of material). Else if dragon material → 100%. Else → 100% (standard).\n   \n   Actually, when enchantment < 8 and not dragon → standard 100%. When enchantment < 8 and dragon → 100% (dragon full). When enchantment ≥ 8 → 50% (whether dragon or not). So effectively: 50% if enchantment ≥ 8, else 100%. The dragon material only matters when... hmm, actually it doesn't change the rate (both dragon<8 and standard are 100%). \n\n   Wait, that doesn't seem right. Let me re-read: \"Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\" and \"Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50 % of the damage amount.\"\n\n   So dragon material → 100%. Enchantment ≥ 8 → 50%. When both apply (dragon + enchantment ≥ 8), \"the 50% rule wins\" → 50%.\n\n   So the precedence: enchantment ≥ 8 takes precedence over dragon. If enchantment ≥ 8 → 50%. Else if dragon → 100%. Else → 100% (standard full).\n\n   So effectively, dragon material gives full reimbursement ONLY when enchantment < 8. And standard (non-dragon, enchantment < 8) is also full. So dragon material never actually changes anything compared to standard? Let me check the examples:\n   - dragon sword, enchantment 5, damage 800 → 700 (full - deductible). Standard non-dragon would also be full → 700. Same.\n   - dragon sword, enchantment 8, damage 1000 → 400 (50% - deductible). \n   - steel sword, enchantment 9, damage 1000 → 400 (50% - deductible). Same as dragon at enchantment 9.\n\n   So dragon material gives the same result as standard in these examples. Then why mention dragon material at all? \n\n   Hmm. Maybe dragon material means NO deductible? Let me re-read: \"Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\" Maybe \"fully reimbursed\" means no deductible? Let me check: dragon sword enchantment 8, damage 1000 → payout 400. If dragon = no deductible and enchantment ≥ 8 = 50%, and 50% wins: 500 (no deductible) = 500? But spec says 400. So deductible DOES apply. So \"fully reimbursed\" doesn't mean no deductible.\n\n   Wait the spec says \"the 50 % rule wins, then deductible: 500 − 100\". So deductible applies. So \"fully reimbursed\" just means 100% of damage (before deductible), as opposed to 50%. So:\n   - enchantment ≥ 8: 50% of damage.\n   - dragon (and enchantment < 8): 100% of damage.\n   - standard (enchantment < 8, not dragon): 100% of damage.\n\n   So dragon and standard are the same (100%). The dragon clause is redundant in effect? Unless... there's a case where standard is NOT 100%. Let me re-read standard: \"regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 G → payout 400 G (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies)\". So standard = full (100%) - deductible. Yes.\n\n   So dragon material = full = same as standard. The dragon clause only matters in contrast to the 50% clause (when both, 50% wins). So effectively the reimbursement rate is: 50% if enchantment ≥ 8, else 100%. Dragon material is a red herring (it's mentioned to clarify that dragon doesn't help against the 50% rule).\n\n   Hmm, but that seems like a weird spec. Let me reconsider. Maybe there's a case where standard is NOT full reimbursement. Let me re-read the claim rules:\n\n   \"### Claim processing\n   A deductible of 100 G applies per damage event. The total payout per policy is capped at twice the insurance sum.\n   - Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50 % of the damage amount.\n   - Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\"\n\n   So the special clauses are: enchantment ≥ 8 → 50%, dragon → full (100%). Standard (no clause) → ? The standard example says \"full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies\". So standard = full - deductible = 100% - deductible. So standard = 100% reimbursement rate.\n\n   So all three (standard, dragon<8, dragon≥8 with 50%winning) ... wait. Let me just define: reimbursement rate = 50% if enchantment ≥ 8 else 100%. Dragon doesn't change the rate (it's 100% when enchantment < 8, same as standard; and when enchantment ≥ 8, 50% wins). So dragon is effectively a no-op in terms of rate. OK. I'll implement: rate = 0.5 if enchantment ≥ 8 else 1.0. And dragon material is tracked but doesn't change the rate (it would only matter if there were a case where dragon < 8 vs standard < 8 differ, which they don't).\n\n   Actually, hmm, wait. Maybe I'm misreading. Let me reconsider: maybe \"fully reimbursed\" for dragon means the deductible is ALSO waived? Let me re-check dragon sword enchantment 5, damage 800 → 700. If dragon = full + no deductible: 800. But spec says 700. So deductible applies. So dragon = full reimbursement rate (100%) but deductible still applies. So 800 - 100 = 700. ✓. So my understanding holds: rate = 100% for dragon (when enchantment < 8), and deductible applies. Standard also 100% with deductible. So same.\n\n   OK so dragon material is effectively a no-op for the rate. But I should still implement it as per the spec (dragon → 100%) because that's the literal rule, even if it equals standard. Actually, since it equals standard, I can just implement rate = 50% if enchantment ≥ 8 else 100%, and ignore dragon. But to be faithful to the spec, maybe: if enchantment ≥ 8, rate = 50%; else if dragon, rate = 100%; else rate = 100%. Same result. I'll do the faithful version.\n\n   Hmm wait, but actually — is there a case where enchantment ≥ 8 AND dragon, and the answer is NOT 50%? The spec says \"both clauses apply; the 50% rule wins\". So 50%. So enchantment ≥ 8 always → 50%, regardless of dragon. OK.\n\n### Multiple items of the same type\n- policy covers two swords → insurance sum 2000 (2×1000), cap 4000.\n- dragon attack damages both swords; damages has two {itemType: \"sword\"} entries → each treated as separate damage with own deductible.\n- if damages array has more entries of a type than policy covers (e.g., two sword damages but only one sword insured) → CLI exits non-zero; whole claim rejected.\n\nSo when processing a claim, the damages must be validated against the policy's items: for each type, the number of damage entries of that type must not exceed the number of insured items of that type. If it does, error (non-zero exit).\n\n### Cap exhaustion\n- policy covers sword + amulet → insurance sum 1600 (1000+600), cap 3200.\n- cursed sword (insurance value 1000, premium with modifiers 165) → cap 2000 (based on unmodified insurance value; premium modifiers don't raise cap).\n- policy covers sword + 3 runes (a block) → insurance sum 1750 (1000 + 3×250); block discount affects premium only, not insurance sum.\n- sword insured (sum 1000, cap 2000); two successive claims of 1500 each:\n  - first claim → payout 1400, cap remaining 600.\n  - second claim → payout 600, cap remaining 0 (desired 1400 reduced to remaining cap).\n\nSo cap = 2 × insurance sum. Insurance sum = sum of insurance values of all items (cursed doesn't change insurance value; block doesn't change insurance value). The cap is reduced by payouts across claims on the same policy.\n\nFor the two successive claims of 1500 each on a sword (cap 2000):\n- First claim: damage 1500. Reimbursement: standard sword (assume steel, enchantment < 8) → full - deductible = 1500 - 100 = 1400. Cap remaining = 2000 - 1400 = 600.\n- Second claim: damage 1500. Desired payout = 1500 - 100 = 1400. But cap remaining is 600, so payout = 600. Cap remaining = 0.\n\nSo payout = min(desired_payout, remaining_cap). And remaining_cap is tracked per policy across claims.\n\n### Rounding in MHPCO's favor\n- premium calculation yielding 197.5 → 198 (round up).\n- payout calculation yielding 350.5 → 350 (round down).\n- intermediate amounts kept as fractions; only final premium/payout rounded.\n\nSo: premium rounds UP (ceil), payout rounds DOWN (floor). Intermediates are fractions.\n\nWait, but the integration examples use whole numbers. Let me check: \"100 G base + 50 G curse + 10 G first insurance = 160 G + 5 G fee = 165 G\". All whole. And the surcharges are percentages that happen to be whole here.\n\nBut consider a 20% loyalty discount on a policy base of, say, 160: 20% of 160 = 32. Whole. Hmm. When would fractions occur? E.g., 30% of 100 = 30 (whole). 50% of 100 = 50. 15% of 100 = 15. 10% of 100 = 10. All whole for 100 base.\n\nBut for other bases, e.g., amulet base 60: 50% curse = 30, 30% high enchant = 18, 20% loyalty = 12, 10% first = 6, 15% follow-up = 9. All whole.\n\nStaff base 80: 50% = 40, 30% = 24, 20% = 16, 10% = 8, 15% = 12. All whole.\n\nPotion base 40: 50% = 20, 30% = 12, 20% = 8, 10% = 4, 15% = 6. All whole.\n\nComponents: 25 base. 50% = 12.5 (fraction!). 30% = 7.5. 20% = 5. 10% = 2.5. 15% = 3.75.\n\nSo with components, fractions arise. E.g., a cursed rune (base 25): curse = 12.5. Total = 25 + 12.5 = 37.5. Then + 5 fee = 42.5 → round up → 43.\n\nHmm wait, but is the curse surcharge applied per-item and then summed, or... Let me re-read: \"item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the base premium of the affected item\". So for each item, compute its base premium, then apply item-specific surcharges to that item's base. Then sum all items' (modified) premiums → policy base premium? \n\nWait, let me re-read the multi-item example: \"a policy covers a cursed sword (base premium 100 G) and a plain amulet (base premium 60 G) → policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G (50 % of the cursed sword's base premium, not of the policy total) → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\".\n\nSo: \n- sword base = 100, cursed → +50. Sword total = 150.\n- amulet base = 60, plain. Amulet total = 60.\n- policy base premium = 100 + 60 = 160? Or 150 + 60 = 210?\n\n\"→ policy base premium 160 G; the cursed surcharge adds 50 G ... → 210 G before further modifiers and fee\". \n\nSo policy base premium = 160 (sum of item base premiums, WITHOUT item-specific modifiers). Then item-specific surcharges are added: cursed +50 → 210. Then policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first, follow-up) apply to the policy base premium (160). Then fee.\n\nWait, that's a bit confusing. Let me re-read carefully:\n\n\"item-specific modifiers (cursed, high enchantment) apply to the base premium of the affected item; policy-wide modifiers (loyalty, first insurance, follow-up contract) apply to the policy base premium (the sum of all item base premiums); the processing fee is added at the very end\"\n\nSo the structure is:\n1. Compute each item's base premium (from type/components/blocks).\n2. policy base premium = sum of item base premiums.\n3. Add item-specific surcharges: for each item with cursed, add 50% of that item's base premium; for each item with enchantment ≥ 5, add 30% of that item's base premium.\n4. Add/subtract policy-wide modifiers: loyalty (−20% of policy base premium if years ≥ 2), first insurance (+10% of policy base premium, always), follow-up (−15% of policy base premium if not first quote).\n5. Add 5 G processing fee.\n6. Round up.\n\nLet me verify with the multi-item example:\n- sword base 100, cursed. amulet base 60, plain.\n- policy base = 100 + 60 = 160.\n- item-specific: cursed sword +50 (50% of 100). Total = 160 + 50 = 210. ✓ (\"→ 210 G before further modifiers and fee\")\n\nAnd the integration newcomer:\n- cursed sword (steel, enchant 3), 0 years, first quote.\n- sword base = 100.\n- policy base = 100.\n- item-specific: curse +50. Total = 150.\n- policy-wide: loyalty 0 (0 < 2), first +10 (10% of 100), follow-up 0 (first quote). Total = 150 + 10 = 160.\n- fee +5 = 165. ✓\n\nLong-standing second contract:\n- cursed sword (steel, enchant 7), 3 years, 2nd quote.\n- sword base = 100.\n- policy base = 100.\n- item-specific: curse +50, high enchant +30 (enchant 7 ≥ 5). Total = 100 + 50 + 30 = 180.\n- policy-wide: loyalty -20 (3 ≥ 2), first +10, follow-up -15. Total = 180 - 20 + 10 - 15 = 155.\n- fee +5 = 160. ✓\n\nSo the order of application: item-specific surcharges are added to the policy base (each based on its own item base), then policy-wide modifiers (each based on policy base premium), then fee. The order of addition/subtraction doesn't matter for the final sum (they're all additive/subtractive on top of policy base). But rounding: intermediates kept as fractions, only final rounded.\n\nSo final premium = policy_base + sum(item_specific_surcharges) + sum(policy_wide_modifiers) + fee, then round up (ceil).\n\nWhere:\n- item_specific for item i = (cursed_i ? 0.5 : 0) × base_i + (enchant_i ≥ 5 ? 0.3 : 0) × base_i.\n- policy_wide = (loyalty ? -0.2 : 0) × policy_base + 0.1 × policy_base (first, always) + (follow_up ? -0.15 : 0) × policy_base.\n\nWait, but is the first insurance always applied? The integration says \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\" So yes, first insurance +10% always. And follow-up -15% when not the first quote in scenario.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider \"first insurance\". The rule says \"A first insurance carries a 10 % initial assessment surcharge.\" And the clarification says each item in a quote is treated as first insurance. So the first insurance surcharge = 10% of policy base premium, always (every quote). \n\nAnd follow-up: \"Customers receive a 15 % discount on each contract after their first.\" So follow-up = -15% of policy base premium, when this is the 2nd+ quote in the scenario.\n\nNow, what determines \"first quote in scenario\"? The steps array. Count quote steps before this one. If 0 before → first quote (no follow-up discount). If ≥1 before → follow-up discount.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Could \"first contract\" mean the first ever, tracked across the customer's history (which we don't have)? The customer object only has yearsWithMHPCO. There's no \"numberOfPreviousContracts\". So the only signal is the position in the scenario's steps. The integration example explicitly says \"this is the customer's second quote in the scenario\" and applies the follow-up discount. So: follow-up discount applies iff this quote step is not the first quote step in the scenario (i.e., there's at least one earlier quote step).\n\nOK. Let me now also reconsider: is the first-insurance surcharge really ALWAYS applied, even on the first quote? Yes — newcomer (first quote) has +10 first insurance. And second contract also has +10 first insurance. So always.\n\nAlright. Now the processing fee: +5 G always (even for empty item list → premium 5).\n\n### Rounding details\n- premium: round up (ceil). 197.5 → 198.\n- payout: round down (floor). 350.5 → 350.\n\nFor premium: final = ceil(policy_base + item_specific + policy_wide + 5). Actually, since 5 is whole, and we round the whole thing up. But wait — should the fee be added before or after rounding? \"intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions; only the final premium or payout is rounded\". So fee is part of the final sum, then round up. So premium = ceil(sum of everything including fee).\n\nHmm, but actually, is the fee itself rounded? No, fee is 5 (whole). So premium = ceil(policy_base + item_specific + policy_wide + 5). Equivalently ceil(policy_base + item_specific + policy_wide) + 5? No! Because ceil(a + 5) = ceil(a) + 5 only if 5 is integer, which it is. ceil(a + 5) = ceil(a) + 5. So same. OK either way.\n\nWait, ceil(a + 5) where 5 is integer: ceil(a + 5) = ceil(a) + 5. Yes. So premium = ceil(policy_base + item_specific + policy_wide) + 5. Same thing. But to be safe, I'll compute the full sum as a fraction then ceil.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether the 5 G fee should be inside the ceil or outside. \"a 5 G processing fee is added to every premium.\" and \"intermediate amounts ... only the final premium ... is rounded\". So the fee is added, then the final is rounded. So premium = ceil(everything including fee). Since fee is whole, it doesn't matter. OK.\n\nHmm, wait, but actually, does rounding happen once at the very end, or could each modifier be rounded? The spec says \"intermediate amounts during a calculation are kept as fractions; only the final premium or payout is rounded.\" So everything is fractional until the final round. Good. So premium = ceil(total_fractional). \n\nBut hold on — let me reconsider the integration example: newcomer premium = 165, all whole. No rounding needed. The rounding example (197.5 → 198) is a separate illustrative example, not tied to a specific scenario. So I need a test that produces a .5 and checks ceil for premium and floor for payout.\n\nFor premium rounding: I need a scenario yielding x.5. E.g., a cursed rune: base 25, curse +12.5, first +2.5 (10% of 25), fee +5 = 25 + 12.5 + 2.5 + 5 = 45.0. Hmm, that's whole. Let me try: cursed rune, 2nd quote (follow-up): base 25, curse +12.5, first +2.5, follow-up -3.75, fee +5 = 25 + 12.5 + 2.5 - 3.75 + 5 = 41.25 → ceil = 42. \n\nHmm, but for the rounding test, the spec gives 197.5 → 198. I don't need to match that exact value; I just need a test that exercises rounding up. But it's cleaner to construct a scenario that yields a known .5 and verify ceil. Actually, the cleanest is to test the rounding behavior directly. But since I'm doing this via the CLI (black-box), I need a scenario. Let me think of one that yields exactly .5.\n\nPremium yielding .5: I need the fractional part to be exactly 0.5. Components give .5 fractions (12.5, 7.5, 2.5). 12.5 + 2.5 = 15 (whole). 12.5 + 7.5 = 20 (whole). 12.5 + 2.5 + 7.5 = 22.5 → .5! So a cursed rune (12.5) with high enchant (7.5) and first insurance (2.5): 25 + 12.5 + 7.5 + 2.5 = 47.5. Plus fee 5 = 52.5 → ceil 53. \n\nHmm wait, but runes — do they have enchantment? The spec says \"runes have no enchantment level or material, so no special clause applies\" (in claim context). For premium, can a rune be cursed or highly enchanted? The schema allows `material`, `enchantment`, `cursed` on any item. But runes \"have no enchantment level or material\". So maybe runes/components ignore enchantment and material and cursed? Or maybe they can be cursed?\n\nHmm, this is ambiguous. The spec says runes have no enchantment level or material (in claim context, \"so no special clause applies\"). For premium, it's unclear if a rune can be cursed. Let me not test cursed/enchanted runes to avoid ambiguity. \n\nFor the premium rounding test, let me find a scenario with .5 that doesn't involve ambiguous rune properties. \n\nMain items have whole-number bases (100, 60, 80, 40) and whole-number surcharges (50%, 30%, 20%, 10%,