um. For sword + 3 runes: sum = 1000 + 750 = 1750, cap = 3500. A single huge damage claim → payout 3500. Good test.\n\n    But wait — the damages must reference items in the policy. If I claim a huge damage on the sword, that's one damage entry {itemType: sword, amount: huge}. desired = huge - 100 (standard, enchant 3 sword). Capped at 3500 → 3500. remainingCap 0. \n\n    Hmm, but actually, does the cap apply per-claim (across all damage entries in the claim) or per-damage-entry? \"The total payout per policy is capped at twice the insurance sum.\" So the total payout (sum of all entries) is capped. And the cap is reduced across claims. So for one claim with multiple entries, the sum is capped. For the huge single damage, payout = min(desired, remainingCap) = 3500.\n\n    OK so test 31: sword + 3 runes, claim huge damage on sword → payout 3500, remainingCap 0. Verifies block doesn't raise insurance sum (cap 3500, not 2×(1000 + 60 block) = ... well block is 60 premium for the 3 runes, insurance sum still 750). Good.\n\n**Edge cases / errors (CLI exit codes):**\n32. Unknown item type in quote ({type: \"broomstick\"}) → CLI exits non-zero, error to stderr, no stdout results.\n33. Claim damage entry for item not in policy (amulet damaged when only sword insured) → exit non-zero, stderr.\n34. Claim damage entry with unknown type → exit non-zero, stderr.\n35. Claim damage entry with negative amount (-200) → exit non-zero, stderr.\n36. Too many damages of a type (two sword damages, only one sword insured) → exit non-zero, stderr, whole claim rejected.\n37. Happy-path CLI: schema example (amulet quote then claim) → stdout has results array with premium and payout/remainingCap. \n    - From the schema example: customer years 5, quote amulet (silver, enchant 2, not cursed), claim amulet damage 200.\n    - Quote: amulet base 60, enchant 2 (< 5, no high enchant), not cursed. policy base 60. loyalty: 5 years ≥ 2 → -12 (20% of 60). first +6 (10% of 60). follow-up 0 (first quote). 