s in order; when I hit a quote, I create a policy and store it (at the step index). When I hit a claim, I look up the policy by `policy` field (which is a step index), validate damages against the policy's items, compute payout, reduce the policy's remaining cap.\n\nFor validating damages: for each damage entry, the itemType must be in the policy, and the count of damage entries per type must not exceed the count of items of that type in the policy. Also amount must be ≥ 0 (negative → error). Also unknown type → error (but if it's unknown, it's not in the policy, so \"item not in policy\" covers it).\n\nLet me also reconsider: the policy stores items (with their properties: material, enchantment, cursed) so that claim processing can look up the item's enchantment and material. But if there are multiple items of a type, and a damage entry references that type, which item's properties (enchantment/material) are used? E.g., two swords, one enchant 8 and one enchant 3. A damage entry {itemType: sword}. Which enchantment? Hmm. The spec example \"two swords\" doesn't specify different enchantments. I'll assume all items of the same type in a policy have the same properties? No — they could differ. But the spec doesn't clarify. The damages array entries only have itemType and amount, not a specific item instance. So how to determine enchantment/material for the rate? \n\nHmm. This is a real ambiguity. Let me re-read: \"a dragon attack damages both swords; damages contains two {itemType: \"sword\", ...} entries → each entry is treated as a separate damage with its own deductible\". So each damage entry is a separate damage event for a sword. But which sword's enchantment/material? \n\nI think the intended model: each damage entry applies to \"a\" sword in the policy, and we need to know that sword's enchantment/material. But the damage entry doesn't specify. So maybe the assumption is that all items of the same type share the same properties (as given in the quote). But the