ue with how I determine \"first insurance\" vs \"follow-up\". Let me re-examine: is the first-insurance surcharge REALLY always applied? The clarification: \"each item in a quote is treated as a first insurance, regardless of customer history.\" Yes, always +10%. And follow-up -15% on 2nd+ quote. So:\n- 1st quote in scenario: +10 first, no follow-up.\n- 2nd+ quote: +10 first, -15 follow-up.\n\nOK. Now, what about a scenario with no quote steps, only claims? Claims reference a policy by index, so there must be a quote step. If a claim references a non-existent policy index, that's an error (probably). The spec doesn't explicitly say, but referencing a policy that doesn't exist should error. I'll handle it (exit non-zero). But I won't add a test for it unless needed. Actually, the spec's error cases are specific; I'll stick to those. But a missing policy index would naturally throw. I'll let it throw (uncaught → non-zero exit). Fine.\n\nNow let me think about the CLI error handling. The CLI must:\n- Read all stdin.\n- Parse JSON. If parse fails or schema invalid → error (exit non-zero, stderr). (Not explicitly tested, but good practice.)\n- Process steps. If any step invalid (unknown type, etc.) → error (exit non-zero, stderr, no stdout).\n- On success → write {results: [...]} to stdout, exit 0.\n\nFor the error tests, I'll spawn the CLI and check exitCode !== 0, stderr non-empty, stdout empty (or not valid results). \n\nLet me now also think: the spec says \"no results are written to stdout\" on error. So stdout should be empty on error. I'll make the CLI buffer results and only write them at the end if all steps succeed. Good.\n\nNow let me reconsider the test count. That's a lot of tests (~39). Let me consolidate and make sure each is distinct and ordered simple→complex. Let me also make sure I cover EVERY example and every clarifying question.\n\nClarifying questions (?):\n1. \"alike\" components — same type or same family? → same type (3 runes + 3 moonstones = two blocks; 2 runes + 1 moonstone = no block). Tests 10, 11 cover this.\n2. Modifier scope on multi-item — whole policy or just item? → just item (cursed surcharge based on cursed item's base). Test 18 covers.\n3. \"first insurance\" — first ever or first time for item? → each item in a quote treated as first insurance. Test 20 covers (2nd contract still has first insurance).\n4. Multiple items of same type — how handled? → each damage separate, too-many → reject. Tests 28, 36 cover.\n\nExamples covered:\n- Building block: 2,3,4,7 runes (tests 6,7,8,9).\n- Alike: 2 runes+1 moonstone, 3 runes+3 moonstones (tests 10, 11).\n- Modifier scope multi-item: cursed sword + amulet (test 18).\n- Modifier thresholds: 2 years loyalty, enchant 5, enchant 4, dragon enchant 8 claim (tests 16, 13/15, 14, 23).\n- Deductible per event: sword+amulet 500+300 → 600 (test 27).\n- Standard reimbursement: regular sword 500→400, rune 200→100 (tests 21, 22).\n- Enchant vs dragon: dragon enchant 9 → 400, dragon enchant 5 → 700, steel enchant 9 → 400 (tests 24, 25, 26).\n- Multiple same type: two swords, too many damages (tests 28, 36).\n- Cap exhaustion: sword two claims 1500 each (test 29).\n- Cursed sword cap (test 30).\n- Sword + 3 runes block cap (test 31).\n- Rounding: 197.5→198 (test 9), 350.5→350 (test 39).\n- Edge: empty list (test 1), unknown type (test 32), damage not in policy (test 33), unknown type in claim (test 34), negative amount (test 35).\n- Integration: newcomer 165 (test 19), long-standing 2nd 160 (test 20), schema example (test 37).\n\nLet me also add: sword+amulet cap 3200? Covered by test 27 (remainingCap 2600 = 3200-600). And the cap example \"policy covers a sword and an amulet → insurance sum 1600, cap 3200\". I'll add a test that directly checks this via a huge damage → payout 3200. Hmm, or rely on test 27. Let me add an explicit cap test to be safe: sword+amulet, huge damage on sword → payout 3200, remainingCap 0. That directly tests cap=3200. Actually, test 27 alrea