16 mass for slightly better DRY/intent. Since Rule 2 (clarity) trumps APP, and the map IS a clearer expression of \"premium depends on item type,\" I'll go with a version of it. But I want to minimize the mass penalty.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether the map is genuinely clearer, because that's the crux.\n\n\"Premium depends on item type, with a default of the processing fee.\"\n\nMap version: reads as \"look up premium by type, default to fee.\" Clear.\nIf version: reads as \"if sword → 115, if amulet → 71, else → 5.\" Also clear, very explicit.\n\nFor 2 items, both are clear. The map's advantage scales with more items. Since the test list shows staff, potion, runes (with block logic!), etc. coming, and the pricing gets complex (blocks, enchantments), the simple type→premium map will NOT capture the full logic anyway — runes use block pricing, swords have enchant/curse surcharges. So the \"map of type→premium\" abstraction will be insufficient and the code will need more. \n\nGiven that, prematurely introducing a flat type→premium map might actually be misleading, because the real logic isn't a simple map. The ifs might evolve into per-type handler functions, not a flat map.\n\nHmm. So the map refactoring is speculative and may not fit the eventual design. Per YAGNI / Rule 4, I should NOT introduce the map yet.\n\nOK so what refactoring IS justified right now?\n\nLet me look really hard at the current code:\n\n```typescript\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst SWORD_PREMIUM = 115;\nconst AMULET_PREMIUM = 71;\n\nexport function runScenario(input: unknown): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const scenario = input as { steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Array<{ type: string }> }> };\n  const step = scenario.steps[0];\n  const itemType = step.items?.[0]?.type;\n  if (itemType === \"sword\") {\n    return { results: [{ premium: SWORD_PREMIUM }] };\n  }\n  if (itemType === \"amulet\") {\n    return { results: [{ premium: AMULET_PREMIUM }] };\n  }\n  return { results: [{ premium: PROCESSING_FEE }] };\n}\n```\n\nPossible small improvements:\n\n(a) The three return statements all construct `{ results: [{ premium: X }] }`. This is repeated 3x. I could extract the result construction OR compute the premium into a single variable and return once. The single-return approach:\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(input: unknown): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const scenario = input as { steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Array<{ type: string }> }> };\n  const step = scenario.steps[0];\n  const itemType = step.items?.[0]?.type;\n  let premium = PROCESSING_FEE;\n  if (itemType === \"sword\") premium = SWORD_PREMIUM;\n  else if (itemType === \"amulet\") premium = AMULET_PREMIUM;\n  return { results: [{ premium }] };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this uses `let` (mutation) and an if/else-if. Mass:\n- `let premium = PROCESSING_FEE`: assignment (6) + binding PROCESSING_FEE (1) + binding premium... = ~7-8\n- `if (itemType === \"sword\") premium = SWORD_PREMIUM`: conditional (4) + binding itemType (1) + const \"sword\" (1) + assignment (6) + binding SWORD_PREMIUM (1) = 13\n- `else if (itemType === \"amulet\") premium = AMULET_PREMIUM`: conditional (4) + binding (1) + const (1) + assignment (6) + binding (1) = 13\n- `return { results: [{ premium }] }`: binding premium (1) = 1\n\nTotal for body: 7(scenario) + 10(step) + 10(itemType) + 8(premium init) + 13 + 13 + 1 = 62. That's WORSE (the assignments are expensive at 6 each, and now I have 3 assignments instead of 1).\n\nSo single-return with `let` is higher mass. Bad.\n\n(b) Keep the if-returns but they're fine as-is.\n\n(c) The `as` cast — define a named input type. Type-level only, zero runtime mass, slight clarity gain. Defensible under Rule 2. But adds an element (the type). Minor.\n\nHonestly, I'm struggling to find a refactoring that clearly improves things without speculation. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the map approach but WITHOUT the `?? \"\"` by using a cleaner def