ive `PREMIUMS[\"undefined\"]` = undefined → falls back. So `?? \"\"` is actually not strictly necessary! Let me check:\n\n`PREMIUMS[undefined]` → in JS, this is `PREMIUMS[\"undefined\"]` which is `undefined` (not in table). Then `?? PROCESSING_FEE` returns the fee. So `PREMIUMS[itemType ?? \"\"] ?? PROCESSING_FEE` could be simplified to `PREMIUMS[itemType ?? \"\"] ?? PROCESSING_FEE`... \n\nHmm wait, but TypeScript typing. `itemType: string | undefined`. `PREMIUMS[itemType]` where itemType is `string | undefined` — TypeScript would complain because `Partial<Record<string, number>>` indexed by `string | undefined`... actually `Record<string, number>` allows any string key. `undefined` as index — TS might error. Let me think. `PREMIUMS` is `Partial<Record<string, number>>`. Indexing with `string | undefined`: TS error because `undefined` isn't a valid index. That's why `itemType ?? \"\"` coerces to string.\n\nSo the `?? \"\"` serves to satisfy TypeScript (coerce undefined → \"\") AND acts as a never-found sentinel. It's doing double duty. It's fine.\n\nCould simplify to: `return PREMIUMS[itemType ?? \"\"] ?? PROCESSING_FEE;` — already minimal.\n\nOr use a Map. No, object is fine.\n\nOK, I'm confident. Let me extract the Scenario type. This is my refactoring. Let me also do a quick sanity: does extracting the type change any runtime behavior? No. Tests stay green.\n\nLet me now also reconsider: maybe instead of (or in addition to) the type, I should make a more impactful change. But the instruction says ONE improvement at a time. I'll do the type extraction.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Actually, let me re-read the type extraction value. The current inline type is:\n```typescript\n{ steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Array<{ type: string }> }> }\n```\n\nIf I extract:\n```typescript\ntype Scenario = { steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Array<{ type: string }> }> };\n```\nand use `input as Scenario`, the `runScenario` body becomes cleaner. But I've just moved the nois