ly, there's an even simpler approach that's exact for this kata: since all amounts are multiples of 0.05, I can round to the nearest 0.05 first (to kill FP error) then ceil. But that's also hacky.\n\nLet me just use the epsilon. Let me define:\n```\nconst EPS = 1e-9;\nfunction roundUpPremium(x: number): number { return Math.ceil(x - EPS); }\nfunction roundDownPayout(x: number): number { return Math.floor(x + EPS); }\n```\n\nWait, ceil(x - EPS): for x=115.0000000001, x-EPS = 114.9999999991, ceil = 115. ✓. For x=197.5, x-EPS=197.499999999, ceil=198. ✓. For x=115 (exact), x-EPS=114.999999999, ceil=115. ✓. For x=115.0 exact (no error), ceil(114.9999) = 115. ✓. Good.\n\nfloor(x + EPS): for x=350.5, x+EPS=350.500000001, floor=350. ✓. For x=350.0000001 (should be 350), floor(350.0000001+EPS)... hmm if x is supposed to be 350 but FP makes it 350.0000001, then floor(350.0000001 + 1e-9) = floor(350.0000001) = 350. ✓. Good.\n\nActually wait, for floor, if the true value is 350.5 (should floor to 350) but FP makes it 350.4999999, then floor(350.4999999 + 1e-9) = floor(350.4999999) = 350. ✓. If true 350.5 FP→350.5000001, floor(350.5000001+1e-9)=350. ✓. Good.\n\nSo the epsilon approach works. Let me use it.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider whether to use the twentieths (exact integer) approach instead, as it's more robust and \"correct\". The refactor subagent values clean code. The epsilon approach has a magic number. The twentieths approach encodes domain knowledge (rates as fractions of 20). Hmm.\n\nLet me think about which is cleaner. The epsilon is a pragmatic FP fix. The twentieths is exact but requires expressing all rates as /20. \n\nActually, you know what, the simplest robust approach: since amounts are multiples of 0.05, I can round to the nearest cent-ish. But let me just use a rounding helper that rounds to, say, 6 decimal places before ceil/floor, to kill FP noise:\n```\nfunction ceilPremium(x) { return Math.ceil(Math.round(x * 1e6) / 1e6); }\n```\nMath.round