is is a Rule 4 (fewest elements) improvement that doesn't hurt Rule 2.\n\nBut \"one improvement at a time\" — naming vs inlining are two different improvements. Hmm.\n\nLet me make a decision. I think the best approach given the experiment's framing (which emphasizes naming FIRST, then APP) is:\n\n**Primary refactoring: Naming (Rule 2)** — rename `itemBase` → `itemBasePremium` and `policyBase` → `policyBasePremium`. This is the first-priority improvement. Mass-neutral; Rule 2 trumps APP.\n\nI'll do JUST this, keep it clean and single-purpose, run tests, and document. This is the most defensible reading of \"naming is first priority\" + \"one improvement at a time\".\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. If I only do a mass-neutral rename, the \"Mass Change\" section will show 0 delta, which is fine and documented (\"Rule 2 trumps APP\"). That's a legitimate outcome. The mission explicitly supports this: \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass\" and the no-improvement case requires detailed explanation, but here there IS an improvement (clarity), just not mass.\n\nHmm, but let me reconsider whether the rename is actually worth doing or if it's gold-plating. `itemBase` with its JSDoc \"Base premium of one quoted item\" — is the name really unclear? In context, `itemBase` returns a number used as a premium component. A reader sees `BASE_PREMIUM` table and `itemBase` returns from it. It's... reasonably clear but `itemBase` literally means \"the base of an item\" which is vague. `itemBasePremium` is unambiguous. I think it's a genuine, if modest, clarity improvement. Given the mandate to attempt at least one refactoring and prioritize naming, this is appropriate.\n\nLet me reconsider one more time if there's a better naming target. `runScenario` returns `{ results: StepResult[] }` and only processes `steps[0]`. The name `runScenario` is fine. `StepResult`, `QuoteResult`, `ClaimResult` — fine. `roundUp` — fine. `BASE_PREMIUM` — fine. `PROCESSING_FEE`, `FIRST_INSURANCE_