pe.\" Many codebases would write it this way.\n\nDecision time. I have two viable refactorings:\n1. Set + keep `isComponent`: clarity win (removes cast), small mass increase (+2).\n2. Set + inline `isComponent`: clarity win (removes cast) + mass decrease (-8), removes a single-use helper.\n\nBoth are defensible. The AGENTS.md / my mission emphasizes Rule 2 (reveals intent) first, then Rule 4 (fewest elements). \n\nFor Rule 2: Is `isComponent(item.type)` or `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)` more intention-revealing? I'd say `isComponent` is marginally more revealing — it directly states the question \"is this a component?\" rather than requiring the reader to connect \"component types has this type\" → \"therefore it's a component.\" But the gap is small.\n\nFor Rule 4: inlining removes a single-use element.\n\nHmm. I think I'll go with option 1 (keep `isComponent`, back with Set). The named predicate is a small, clear abstraction that reads well at the call site, and Rule 2 (intent) is higher priority than Rule 4 (fewest elements). The cast removal is the real win.\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider. Single-use helpers are a common target for inlining, and `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(item.type)` is genuinely clear. But there's also a counter-argument: `isComponent` decouples the \"what\" (is it a component) from the \"how\" (membership in COMPONENT_TYPES set). If later the definition of component changes, only `isComponent` changes. That's a mild abstraction benefit. But YAGNI.\n\nI'll commit to option 1: replace the array+cast with a Set, keep `isComponent`. Clean, defensible, removes a TypeScript workaround. Mass +2 justified by Rule 2.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider one more time. A +2 mass increase with the justification \"removes an awkward cast\" — is that cast really obscuring intent enough to justify it? The cast is purely a TypeScript type-narrowing workaround. A reader who knows TS understands it. But it IS noise. The Set is the idiomatic solution. I'm