t(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThe counting line `componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;` is a bit dense. Could use a helper or a different structure. But it's standard count-accumulation. \n\nAlternative using a Map? No, Record is fine.\n\nI don't see a clearly-better structural change that doesn't add elements. The code is genuinely fairly clean already.\n\nSo my refactoring is the Set change. Let me finalize and apply it. I'll document the +2 mass honestly and justify via Rule 2.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider. I keep going back and forth. Let me just make a clean decision and also reconsider if combining the Set change with keeping isComponent, OR doing the inline, is better.\n\nI realize there's actually a cleaner framing: The real issue is the awkward cast. The Set fixes that. Whether to keep `isComponent` is a separate, minor stylistic choice. To minimize my footprint and keep the change focused (one improvement), I'll do exactly: replace array+cast with Set, keeping everything else identical. This is the minimal, focused refactoring.\n\nFinal: Set-backed COMPONENT_TYPES, keep isComponent (now `COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type)`).\n\nLet me apply it.\n\nLet me write the edit:\n\n```typescript\n/** Components are insured at a flat per-item value; a block of 3 alike is offered cheaper. */\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\nconst COMPONENT_BASE = 25;\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst isComponent = (type: string): boolean => COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type);\n```\n\nReplacing:\n```typescript\n/** Components are insured at a flat per-item value; a block of 3 alike is offered cheaper. */\nconst COM