o 11 vs 6 — an increase of 5. Plus the `base` binding in the const line (1) ... \n\nHmm so by pure mass, extracting the variable INCREASES mass because of the assignment cost. This is a case where APP mass would say \"don't refactor\" but Rule 2 (reveals intent) says the explaining variable improves clarity. The guidance explicitly says \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass.\"\n\nSo I have a tension. Let me reconsider whether the refactoring actually improves clarity enough to justify the mass increase.\n\nThe benefit of extracting `base`:\n1. Avoids calling `itemBaseValue(item)` twice (DRY - the knowledge of \"how to get the base\" is invoked once).\n2. Makes explicit that both surcharges are computed from the same base value (intent-revealing).\n\nBut mass increases by ~6.\n\nGiven \"Rule 2 trumps APP\", and the DRY benefit is real (single representation of the base-value computation call), I think this is justified. But let me consider whether there's a refactoring that both improves clarity AND reduces mass.\n\nHmm, what about Option B (sum rates, multiply once)?\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  let rate = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) rate += CURSE_RATE;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) rate += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE;\n  return rate * base;\n}\n```\n\nMass:\n- `item` param: 1\n- `const base = itemBaseValue(item)`: binding base 1 + invocation 2 + binding item 1 + assignment 6 = 10\n- `let rate = 0`: assignment 6 + constant 0 1 = 7\n- `if (item.cursed) rate += CURSE_RATE`: conditional 4 + binding item 1 + assignment 6 + binding CURSE_RATE 1 = 12\n- `if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) rate += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE`: conditional 4 + binding item 1 + conditional ?? 4 + constant 0 1 + binding HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD 1 + assignment 6 + binding HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE 1 = 18\n- `return rate * base`: binding rate 1 + binding base 1 = 2\nTotal: 1 + 10 + 7 + 12 + 18 + 2 = 50\n\nSo Option B: 50, vs original 45. Still an increase of 5.\n\nBoth options increase mass because of the assignment cost of the extracted variable. But both improve clarity/DRY.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Is there a refactoring that genuinely reduces mass?\n\nLet me look elsewhere in the code.\n\n`policyBasePremium`:\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis has a conceptual issue: the block discount only applies when count === BLOCK_SIZE exactly. For 4 runes, count=4, so it does count * COMPONENT_BASE = 100. Test \"4 runes -> premium 115\" = 100 + 10 + 5 = 115. Correct.\n\nBut the block logic: `count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE`. This means only EXACTLY 3 gets the block discount. That's the current behavior per tests. Not a refactoring target since tests define it.\n\nIs there duplication here? The `componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1` pattern is a standard count accumulator. Not really duplicated elsewhere.\n\nHmm, what about `itemBaseValue`:\n```typescript\nfunction itemBaseValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_BASE : BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n```\nThis is clean. Note it calls `isComponent(item.type)` and `policyBasePremium` also calls `isComponent(item.type)`. There's some conceptual coupling but not obvious duplication.\n\nLet me reconsider. The task mandates at least one refactoring attempt. The best candidate is the `itemSurcharges` DRY improvement. Even though mass increases slightly, Rule 2 (reveals intent) trumps APP, and the DRY benefit (single call to `itemBaseValue`) is legitimate.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the mass calculation. I may be over-counting. Let me reconsider whether `item` (the parameter) should be counted each time it's referenced. \n\nLooking at the APP guidance: \"Binding/Scalar (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters (`amount`, `result`)\". Each *occurrence* of a variable/parameter is a binding usage. So yes, each reference to `item` counts as 1.\n\nHmm, but actually that makes mass heavily influenced by variable reuse, which would penalize extracting variables. That seems intended — APP rewards fewer elements and direct computation. But the guidance explicitly says Rule 2 trumps APP, and explaining variables are a Rule 2 tool.\n\nOK. Let me reconsider the whole picture. Given:\n- The `itemSurcharges` refactor improves DRY (single `itemBaseValue` call) and reveals intent (both surcharges are fractions of one base).\n- Mass increases modestly (~5-6) due to the assignment cost.\n- Rule 2 trumps APP.\n\nI'll go with Option A (the more conservative one that keeps the additive money structure). Actually, let me reconsider A vs B once more for intent.\n\nOption A:\n```typescript\nconst base = itemBaseValue(item);\nlet surcharge = 0;\nif (item.cursed) surcharge += CURSE_RATE * base;\nif ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) surcharge += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base;\nreturn surcharge;\n```\n\nOption B:\n```typescript\nconst base = itemBaseValue(item);\nlet rate = 0;\nif (item.cursed) rate += CURSE_RATE;\nif ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) rate += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE;\nreturn rate * base;\n```\n\nOption B is more intent-revealing: it makes explicit the structure \"surcharge = (sum of applicable risk rates) × base value\". This is a cleaner mental model. And it has slightly lower mass than A (50 vs 51) because the two `* base` multiplications are folded into one.\n\nI think Option B is the better refactoring. It