e adds, the two discounts subtract). \n\nOne possible refactoring: extract a helper that computes a \"policy-wide adjustment\". But the conditional nature of two of them and the differing signs make this tricky. Let me think about whether it actually improves things.\n\nActually, I think there's a cleaner approach. Let me think about the structure of `quotePremium`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction quotePremium(customer, items, isFollowUp): number {\n  const policyBase = policyBasePremium(items);\n  const surcharges = items.reduce((sum, it) => sum + itemSurcharges(it), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  return roundUp(\n    policyBase + surcharges + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThe final return is: `policyBase + surcharges + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE`.\n\nThis is quite clear actually. Each modifier is named and visible.\n\n### Rule 4: Fewest Elements\n\nLet me look for unnecessary abstractions or simplifications.\n\n1. The `quoteCount` tracking in `runScenario`:\n```typescript\nlet quoteCount = 0;\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowUp = quoteCount > 0;\n      quoteCount += 1;\n      ...\n    }\n```\n\nCould I use a boolean `hasQuotedBefore` instead of a count? Since we only ever check `quoteCount > 0`, a boolean would be more intention-revealing:\n\n```typescript\nlet hasQuoted = false;\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowUp = hasQuoted;\n      hasQuoted = true;\n      ...\n    }\n```\n\nWait, but is `quoteCount` going to be needed for more than just the follow-up check in future tests? Looking at the todo list:\n- \"quote: long-standing 2nd contract cursed enchant 7 -> 1st 95, 2nd 160\" - this is still just 2nd quote, follow-up.\n\nThe follow-up discount only cares about whether it's the first quote or not. So a boolean `hasQuoted` is more intention-revealing than a count. This is a Rule 2 (reveals intent) AND Rule 4 (fewest elements) improvement.\n\nActually wait - using `quoteCount` (a number) when we only need a boolean is a mild over-abstraction. The count suggests we care about how many quotes there have been, but we only care about whether there's been at least one prior. A boolean `hasQuoted` (or `seenQuote`) better reveals intent.\n\nBut hold on - let me reconsider. Is there any future test that might need the count? Let me scan the todos again:\n- All follow-up related tests just distinguish 1st vs 2nd (boolean).\n- No test needs \"3rd quote\" or counts.\n\nSo a boolean is cleaner. Let me make this change. This improves Rule 2 (reveals intent: \"has a quote happened yet\" vs \"how many quotes\") and Rule 4 (boolean is simpler than counter).\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether this is truly an improvement. `quoteCount > 0` is also clear. And `quoteCount` is arguably more future-proof. But the principle of \"fewest elements\" and \"reveals intent\" suggests we should model exactly what we need. We need \"is this the first quote?\" A boolean captures that precisely.\n\nHmm, but actually there's a subtlety. Let me re-examine. `quoteCount` increments only on quote steps. After first quote, `quoteCount = 1`, so `quoteCount > 0` is true → isFollowUp = true. Correct.\n\nWith boolean:\n```typescript\nlet hasQuoted = false;\nfor (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFollowUp = hasQuoted;\n      hasQuoted = true;\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(scenario.customer, step.items ?? [], isFollowUp) });\n    }\n```\n\nThis is equivalent. The boolean version more directly expresses \"is this a follow-up? = has there been a quote before?\".\n\nLet me calculate