s.\n\n`quoteCount` version (relevant lines):\n- `let quoteCount = 0;` — 1 binding, 1 const, 1 assignment\n- `const isFollowUp = quoteCount > 0;` — 1 binding (isFollowUp), 1 binding (quoteCount), 1 comparison op (invocation? 2), 1 const (0), 1 assignment\n- `quoteCount += 1;` — 1 binding (quoteCount), 1 const (1), 1 assignment\n\n`hasQuoted` (boolean) version:\n- `let hasQuoted = false;` — 1 binding, 1 const (false), 1 assignment\n- `const isFollowUp = hasQuoted;` — 1 binding (isFollowUp), 1 binding (hasQuoted), 1 assignment\n- `hasQuoted = true;` — 1 binding (hasQuoted), 1 const (true), 1 assignment\n\nThe difference: the boolean version removes the `> 0` comparison (1 invocation, mass 2) and the `0` constant. So it reduces mass by ~3.\n\nSo the boolean version is lower mass AND arguably clearer intent (\"has a quote been seen\" vs \"count of quotes, check if > 0\").\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider once more. Is `quoteCount` perhaps kept intentionally because future claim cycles might need it? Looking at the todo tests, claims deal with caps and payouts based on items, not quote counts. The follow-up concept is purely about \"is this the first quote\". So boolean is appropriate.\n\nBut I want to be careful — is this \"over-refactoring\"? The TDD principle is to write code for the current tests, not future ones. Currently, we only need \"first vs follow-up\". A boolean models this exactly. So replacing the counter with a boolean aligns with Rule 4 (fewest elements) and Rule 2 (reveals intent).\n\nLet me make this change. It's a clean, safe, intention-revealing refactoring.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether there are OTHER, possibly more impactful refactorings.\n\nLet me look at `policyBasePremium`:\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis separates components (counted then priced in blocks) from non-components (priced immediately). The two-loop structure is because components need their total count before applying the block discount.\n\nThis is reasonably clear. There's a subtle duplication: `isComponent(item.type)` is checked here AND in `itemBaseValue`. But that's a minor conceptual overlap, and removing it might hurt clarity.\n\nHmm, actually `itemBaseValue` is:\n```typescript\nfunction itemBaseValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_BASE : BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n```\n\nAnd in `policyBasePremium`, components are NOT added via `itemBaseValue` (they're counted and block-discounted), while non-components ARE added via `itemBaseValue`. So `itemBaseValue` returns `COMPONENT_BASE` for components, but that path is only used in `itemSurcharges` (via `const base = itemBaseValue(item)`). \n\nWait, let me check `itemSurcharges`:\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += CURSE_RATE * base;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) surcharge += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base;\n  return surcharge;\n}\n```\n\nSo for a component item, `itemBaseValue` returns `COMPONENT_BASE` (25), and surcharges apply to that. But the policy base for components uses block pricing. Is that a bug? Let me check the tests... The tests with components (runes, moonstones) don't have cursed/enchantment, so surcharges for components are never tested. This is fine for now (TDD — don't fix untested behavior).\n\nOK so I don't want to touch the component logic since it's not tested with surcharges.\n\nLet me focus on the cleaner