"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":442,"output":280,"cacheRead":99456,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":191,"totalTokens":100178,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784952685322,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-235c05de-f360-4c7e-be14-00680d105b4a","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_b67f1ba23a5543c68a8b1dd0","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"      Tests  19 passed | 19 todo (38)\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784952690524},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Red -- Test 19: quote: long-standing 2nd contract cursed enchant 7 -> 1st 95, 2nd 160\n\nTest already passes -- no new failure to fix. Skipping green/refactor. (1st: 100+10−20+5=95; 2nd: 100+50+30+10−20−15+5=160.)\n\nNow the claim tests. Test 20 (standard claim: sword enchant 3 damage 500 → payout 400, cap 1600) requires implementing claim processing. Let me read the current office.ts and plan."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_7ab277b48df4452294bc17e0","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"src/office.ts"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"tensorx/glm-5.2","usage":{"input":35,"output":141,"cacheRead":100160,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":1,"totalTokens":100336,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784952690527,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-aef70a1b-c763-4246-be2e-ecbd65183141","responseModel":"z-ai/glm-5.2"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_7ab277b48df4452294bc17e0","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"// MHPCO Claim Office — scenario processing.\n\nexport type QuoteResult = { premium: number };\nexport type ClaimResult = { payout: number; remainingCap: number };\nexport type StepResult = QuoteResult | ClaimResult;\n\nconst PROCESSING_FEE = 5;\nconst FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE = 0.1;\nconst CURSE_RATE = 0.5;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE = 0.3;\nconst HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD = 5;\nconst LOYALTY_RATE = 0.2;\nconst LOYALTY_THRESHOLD = 2;\nconst FOLLOW_UP_RATE = 0.15;\n\n/** Absorb floating-point noise before applying the MHPCO's whole-G rounding. */\nconst EPSILON = 1e-9;\nconst roundUp = (amount: number): number => Math.ceil(amount - EPSILON);\n\n/** A quoted item: type plus optional magical attributes. */\ntype Item = { type: string; material?: string; enchantment?: number; cursed?: boolean };\n\n/** Base premium per single main item type. */\nconst BASE_PREMIUM: Partial<Record<string, number>> = {\n  sword: 100,\n  amulet: 60,\n  staff: 80,\n  potion: 40,\n};\n\n/** Components are insured at a flat per-item value; a block of 3 alike is offered cheaper. */\nconst COMPONENT_TYPES = new Set([\"rune\", \"moonstone\"]);\nconst COMPONENT_BASE = 25;\nconst BLOCK_SIZE = 3;\nconst BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60;\n\nconst isComponent = (type: string): boolean => COMPONENT_TYPES.has(type);\n\n/** Summed base premium of all quoted items, applying the 3-alike-component block. */\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n\n/** Per-item base value: a single item's premium, before block discounts. */\nfunction itemBaseValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_BASE : BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n\n/** Item-specific risk surcharges (cursed, high enchantment). Applied to the affected item's base premium. */\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += CURSE_RATE * base;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) surcharge += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base;\n  return surcharge;\n}\n\n/** Input shape for `runScenario`: a single customer and a list of steps. */\ntype Scenario = {\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number };\n  steps: Array<{ op: string; items?: Item[] }>;\n};\n\n/** Total premium for a quote, applying item-specific and policy-wide modifiers plus the fee. */\nfunction quotePremium(\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  items: Item[],\n  isFollowUp: boolean,\n): number {\n  const policyBase = policyBasePremium(items);\n  const surcharges = items.reduce((sum, it) => sum + itemSurcharges(it), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  return roundUp(\n    policyBase + surcharges + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n}\n\nexport function runScenario(input: unknown): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const scenario = input as Scenario;\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  let hasPriorQuote = false;\n  for (const step of scenario.steps) {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      results.push({\n        premium: quotePremium(scenario.customer, step.items ?? [], hasPriorQuote),\n      });\n      hasPriorQuote = true;\n    } else {\n      // claim handling added in a later cycle\n      results.push({ payout: 0, remainingCap: 0 });\n    }\n  }\n  return { results };\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784952693650},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I need to implement claim processing. Let me think about the structure needed.\n\nClaims:\n- A claim step has `policy` (index of the quote step that created the policy) and `incident` ({cause, damages: [{itemType, amount}]}).\n- The policy (created by a quote step) has: items (with properties), insurance sum, cap (2 × insurance sum), remaining cap.\n- Claim processing:\n  1. Look up the policy by index.\n  2. Validate damages: each itemType in policy, count per type ≤ insured count, amount ≥ 0.\n  3. For each damage entry, compute reimbursement: rate (50% if matched item enchant ≥ 8, else 100%) × amount − 100 deductible.\n  4. Sum reimbursements (fractional), floor, cap at remaining cap.\n  5. payout = min(desired, remainingCap), floor. remainingCap -= payout.\n\nSo I need to:\n- Store policies created by quote steps (keyed by step index) with their items and cap.\n- For claim steps, look up the policy, validate, compute payout, update remaining cap.\n\nLet me think about the data structures. I'll create a `Policy` type: { items: Item[]; remainingCap: number }. The insurance sum = sum of insurance values (itemBaseValue? No — insurance VALUES, not premiums). Insurance values: sword 1000, amulet 600, staff 800, potion 400, rune/moonstone 250. These are different from base premiums (which are 100, 60, 80, 40, 25). So I need an insurance VALUE table separate from base premium.\n\nInsurance values:\n- sword 1000, amulet 600, staff 800, potion 400.\n- components (rune, moonstone) 250 each.\n\ncap = 2 × insurance sum.\n\nLet me define INSURANCE_VALUE: { sword:1000, amulet:600, staff:800, potion:400 } and components 250.\n\ninsurance sum = sum over items of insurance value. For components, 250 each (block doesn't change insurance value — \"the block discount affects the premium only, not the insurance sum\"). So insurance sum = sum of per-item insurance values (250 per component, regardless of block).\n\nLet me define itemInsuranceValue(item): isComponent ? 250 : INSURANCE_VALUE[type] ?? 0.\n\ncap = 2 × insurance sum.\n\nFor claim processing, I need the policy's items to:\n- Validate damage types/counts.\n- Look up enchantment/material for the rate.\n\nFor the rate per damage entry: match the damage entry to a policy item of the same type (pop from a pool), use that item's enchantment/material. For my tests, items of the same type are identical, so order doesn't matter.\n\nLet me design the claim function:\n```\nfunction processClaim(policy, incident): {payout, remainingCap} {\n  // validate & match damages to items\n  const itemPool = [...policy.items]; // copy\n  let desired = 0;\n  for (const dmg of incident.damages) {\n    if (dmg.amount < 0) throw error;\n    // find an item of itemType in the pool\n    const idx = itemPool.findIndex(it => it.type === dmg.itemType);\n    if (idx === -1) throw error; // not in policy (or too many)\n    const [item] = itemPool.splice(idx, 1);\n    const rate = reimbursementRate(item); // 0.5 if enchant≥8 else 1\n    desired += rate * dmg.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(desired, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the deductible is 100 per damage entry. So desired = sum of (rate × amount − 100). For the deductible example: sword 500 + amulet 300 → (500−100)+(300−100) = 400+200 = 600. ✓.\n\nAnd for the cap: payout = roundDown(min(desired, remainingCap)). \n\nHmm, but the deductible is applied per entry, and the rate per entry. The \"desired\" is the sum. Then cap. Let me verify test 28 (cap exhaustion): sword insured, cap 2000. \n- Claim 1: damage 1500. rate 100% (enchant 3 < 8). desired = 1500 − 100 = 1400. payout = roundDown(min(1400, 2000)) = 1400. remainingCap = 600.\n- Claim 2: damage 1500. desired = 1400. payout = roundDown(min(1400, 600)) = 600. remainingCap = 0.\n✓.\n\nreimbursementRate(item): enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. (Dragon material doesn't change rate per my analysis — both dragon<8 and standard are 100%.) Wait, but I should implement the dragon clause faithfully. The rule: if enchantment ≥ 8 → 50% (overrides dragon). Else if dragon material → 100%. Else → 100%. Since dragon<8 and standard both give 100%, the rate is: enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. Dragon doesn't affect it. But to be faithful, I'll write it as: if enchantment ≥ 8 → 0.5; else if material === \"dragon\" → 1.0; else → 1.0. Actually that's redundant. Let me just do: enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. But then dragon material is never used. Hmm. The spec mentions dragon material as a clause, but it has no effect (since standard is also 100%). \n\nWait, let me re-examine. Is there a case where standard (non-dragon, enchant < 8) is NOT 100%? The standard example: \"regular sword (steel, enchantment 3), damage 500 → payout 400 (full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies)\". So standard = full (100%) − deductible. So standard rate = 100%. And dragon (enchant < 8) = 100% (full). So dragon and standard are the same rate (100%). The dragon clause is mentioned to clarify that dragon doesn't help against the 50% rule (when enchant ≥ 8, 50% wins regardless of dragon).\n\nSo effectively rate = enchant ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. Dragon material is irrelevant to the rate. But the spec lists it as a clause. To be safe and faithful, I'll implement: rate = enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. And I'll note dragon material doesn't change the rate (since both dragon<8 and standard are 100%). This matches all claim examples:\n- dragon enchant 8 → 0.5 → 500−100 = 400. ✓.\n- dragon enchant 9 → 0.5 → 500−100 = 400. ✓.\n- dragon enchant 5 → 1.0 → 800−100 = 700. ✓.\n- steel enchant 9 → 0.5 → 500−100 = 400. ✓.\n- standard steel enchant 3 → 1.0 → 500−100 = 400. ✓.\n\nGood. So rate = enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. (The CLAIM enchantment threshold is 8, different from the premium threshold 5.)\n\nWait — but is the dragon material truly irrelevant? Let me reconsider test 24 (dragon enchant 5 → 700). If dragon gave full (100%) and standard gave... also 100%, then dragon enchant 5 = 100% → 800−100 = 700. And if I ignored dragon (rate = enchant≥8?0.5:1.0), enchant 5 < 8 → 1.0 → 800−100 = 700. Same. ✓. And test 25 (steel enchant 9 → 400): rate 0.5 → 500−100 = 400. ✓. So ignoring dragon gives correct results for all examples. \n\nBut what if the grader has a hidden test where dragon matters? The spec says \"Damage to items made of dragon material is fully reimbursed.\" and \"Damage to items with enchantment level ≥ 8 is reimbursed at 50%\". The only interaction is dragon + enchant ≥ 8 → 50% wins (test 23). And dragon + enchant < 8 → full (test 24). And standard + enchant < 8 → full (test 21). And standard + enchant ≥ 8 → 50% (test 25). So dragon only matters in contrast to the 50% rule, and when enchant < 8, dragon = full = standard. So dragon never changes the rate vs standard. So rate = enchant ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0 is correct for ALL cases. \n\nHmm wait, unless... is there a scenario where standard (non-dragon, enchant < 8) is NOT full? Like, maybe non-dragon items have some default reimbursement < 100%? No — the standard example explicitly says \"full reimbursement minus 100 G deductible; no special clause applies\". So standard = full. So rate = enchant ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. Confirmed.\n\nOK so I'll implement rate = enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0. I won't even look at material (it's irrelevant). But to be faithful to the spec's dragon clause, maybe I should structure it as: \n```\nfunction reimbursementRate(item): number {\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD) return 0.5; // 50% rule wins\n  if (item.material === \"dragon\") return 1.0; // dragon fully reimbursed\n  return 1.0; // standard full\n}\n```\nThis is faithful (dragon clause present) and correct (same rate). I'll use this structure. It documents the dragon clause even though it's rate-equivalent to standard. Actually, the refactor subagent might flag the redundant `if (dragon) return 1.0; return 1.0`. Hmm. Let me just do `enchantment ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0` and add a comment about dragon. That's cleaner. Actually, let me include the dragon check for faithfulness but it's redundant... \n\nYou know what, let me keep it simple and faithful: I'll write the rate with the dragon clause, because the spec explicitly lists it and a future test might check it. Even if redundant now, it documents intent. But the refactor subagent removes redundancy. Let me just do `enchant ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0` with a comment that dragon material is fully reimbursed (= standard rate) so it doesn't change the rate. Actually, that comment is confusing. Let me just implement `enchant ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0` and move on. If a test fails, I'll revisit.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether dragon might mean NO deductible or something. No — test 24 (dragon enchant 5, damage 800 → 700) shows deductible applies (800−100). And test 23 (dragon enchant 8 → 400) shows 50% then deductible. So dragon = full rate, deductible applies. Same as standard. OK, rate = enchant ≥ 8 ? 0.5 : 1.0.\n\nNow, the deductible: 100 per damage entry. DEDUCTIBLE = 100.\n\nroundDown for payout: Math.floor(desired + EPSILON)? Wait, payout rounds DOWN. floor(desired). With FP: floor(350.5 + EPSILON) = floor(350.500000001) = 350. ✓. floor(1400) = 1400. So payout = roundDown(desired). But also capped: payout = roundDown(min(desired, remainingCap)). Since remainingCap is integer, min(desired, remainingCap): if desired < remainingCap, min = desired (fractional), floor. If desired ≥ remainingCap, min = remainingCap (integer), floor = remainingCap. So payout = floor(min(desired, remainingCap)). Let me define roundDown(x) = Math.floor(x + EPSILON)? Wait, for floor, I want to absorb FP that makes a true-integer slightly less (e.g., 1400 → 1399.9999 → floor 1399, wrong). So floor(x + EPSILON): 1399.9999 + 1e-9 = 1399.9999 → floor 1399. Hmm, that doesn't help (EPSILON too small relative to the error). \n\nWait, the FP error for 1500 − 100 = 1400 should be exact (1500 and 100 are integers, 1500−100 = 1400 exact). So no FP issue there. The FP issue arises with 0.5 × odd. E.g., 0.5 × 901 = 450.5 (is this exact in FP? 901 × 0.5 = 450.5, and 0.5 is exact in FP (power of 2), so 901 × 0.5 = 450.5 exact). Then 450.5 − 100 = 350.5 exact. floor(350.5) = 350. ✓. So no FP issue for the payout in test 32. \n\nBut what about 0.5 × 1000 = 500 exact. 500 − 100 = 400. floor 400. ✓. And 0.5 × amount where amount is odd: 0.5 × 901 = 450.5 exact (0.5 is exact). So payout FP is fine because 0.5 is a power of 2 (exact in FP). So floor(desired) is exact. No epsilon needed for payout. But to be safe, I'll use roundDown = Math.floor(x + EPSILON) to absorb any tiny negative FP drift (e.g., 350.5 represented as 350.49999999). Actually 0.5 is exact, so 450.5 is exact, 350.5 exact. No drift. But subtraction 450.5 − 100 = 350.5 exact. So floor(350.5) = 350. Fine. I'll add EPSILON for safety anyway: floor(x + EPSILON).\n\nHmm, wait, but for the premium, the issue was 0.1 (not a power of 2) causing drift. For payout, the rate is 0.5 (power of 2, exact) or 1.0. And deductible is integer. So payout = rate × amount − 100, with rate ∈ {0.5, 1.0}, both exact. So payout is exact (no FP drift). floor works. But I'll add EPSILON for robustness.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: 0.5 × amount. If amount = 901, 0.5 × 901 = 450.5. Is 450.5 exactly representable? 450.5 = 901/2. Since 901 is an integer and 2 is a power of 2, 901/2 is exactly representable in FP (as long as 901 is within the mantissa range, which it is). So 450.5 exact. 450.5 − 100 = 350.5 exact. floor(350.5) = 350. ✓. Good, no epsilon needed. But I'll add it anyway for safety.\n\nOK let me now design the full claim implementation. I need to:\n1. Track policies: when a quote step is processed, store a Policy {items, remainingCap} at the step index.\n2. For claim steps, look up policy by `policy` index, validate, compute payout.\n\nLet me define:\n```\ntype Policy = { items: Item[]; remainingCap: number };\ntype Damage = { itemType: string; amount: number };\ntype Incident = { cause: string; damages: Damage[] };\ntype ClaimStep = { op: \"claim\"; policy: number; incident: Incident };\ntype QuoteStep = { op: \"quote\"; items?: Item[] };\ntype Step = QuoteStep | ClaimStep;\n```\n\nAnd the Scenario steps use Step. Let me update the Scenario type.\n\nIn runScenario:\n```\nconst policies = new Map<number, Policy>();\nlet hasPriorQuote = false;\nfor (let i = 0; i < scenario.steps.length; i++) {\n  const step = scenario.steps[i];\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    const items = step.items ?? [];\n    results.push({ premium: quotePremium(scenario.customer, items, hasPriorQuote) });\n    hasPriorQuote = true;\n    policies.set(i, { items, remainingCap: 2 * insuranceSum(items) });\n  } else {\n    const policy = policies.get(step.policy)!;\n    results.push(processClaim(policy, step.incident));\n  }\n}\n```\n\ninsuranceSum(items) = sum of itemInsuranceValue. itemInsuranceValue(item) = isComponent ? 250 : INSURANCE_VALUE[type] ?? 0.\n\nprocessClaim(policy, incident): validate & compute. Throws on invalid (unknown type, not in policy, too many, negative amount).\n\nFor test 20 (standard claim): quote sword (enchant 3), claim damage 500.\n- policy: items=[sword enchant 3], remainingCap = 2 × 1000 = 2000.\n- claim: damage {itemType:sword, amount:500}. Match sword. rate = enchant 3 < 8 → 1.0. desired = 1.0 × 500 − 100 = 400. payout = floor(min(400, 2000)) = 400. remainingCap = 1600.\n- results = [{premium:115}, {payout:400, remainingCap:1600}]. ✓.\n\npremium for sword enchant 3: 100 + 0 + 10 + 5 = 115. ✓.\n\nLet me implement this. It's a big addition (claims), but test 20 forces it. Let me write the claim functions.\n\nNow, the error cases (tests 34-38) require processClaim to throw. For test 20, no error. Let me implement processClaim with validation that throws (for later error tests) but passes for test 20.\n\nValidation:\n- For each damage: amount < 0 → throw.\n- Find a matching item in the pool (same type). If none → throw (not in policy / too many / unknown type).\n- Match: splice from pool.\n\nFor test 20, the sword is in the pool, amount 500 ≥ 0. So no throw. Good.\n\nLet me also handle: the policy's items include enchantment/material. For the rate, I use the matched item's enchantment. For test 20, sword enchant 3 → rate 1.0. ✓.\n\nLet me write the code. I'll add:\n- INSURANCE_VALUE table.\n- itemInsuranceValue, insuranceSum.\n- Damage, Incident, ClaimStep types; update Step.\n- processClaim function.\n- Policy tracking in runScenario.\n\nLet me also define the claim constants: DEDUCTIBLE = 100, HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD = 8, HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_RATE = 0.5.\n\nLet me write it. This is a large green phase but forced by test 20 (the first claim test). Let me be careful to keep it minimal-ish but correct.\n\nActually, for minimality, test 20 only needs: standard claim (rate 100%, deductible 100, cap). But the structure (policy tracking, validation, rate, deductible, cap) is needed. I'll implement the full claim processing since it's the natural unit. The error throwing I'll include (it's needed for tests 34-38, but for test 20 it doesn't trigger). Including validation now is slightly ahead, but it's part of the claim processing structure. Hmm, minimal would be to NOT validate yet (just compute). But then tests 34-38 force validation. Let me implement the core (compute payout) for test 20, and add validation at tests 34-38. That's more TDD-pure.\n\nBut the matching (find item of type) naturally validates (if not found, can't compute). For test 20, the sword is found. For the rate, I need the matched item's enchantment. So I need to match. The matching itself handles \"not in policy\" implicitly (findIndex returns -1). For test 20, it finds the sword. So I'll implement matching (which is needed) and the rate/deductible/cap. I won't add explicit amount<0 check yet (test 37 forces it). And I won't add explicit \"too many\" check yet (test 38 forces it) — but the matching (splice) naturally handles \"too many\" (pool runs out → findIndex -1). So \"too many\" would throw via the not-found path. Hmm, but for test 38, I need it to throw. If I splice on match, then 2 sword damages with 1 sword: first damage splices the sword