row new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    desired += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(desired, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\nNew:\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let desiredPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    desiredPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(desiredPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nI can merge edits 1 and 2 are in different locations but I can do them as separate oldText entries in one edit call. Edit 3 touches processClaim — the `let desired = 0;`, `desired += ...`, and `Math.min(desired, ...)` are three separate spots but within the same function. I'll do them as separate oldText entries too. Let me combine all into one edit call with multiple entries.\n\nActually, edits 1 and 2 are adjacent-ish (both in the claim-processing constants/area but separated by other code). Let me just do them as distinct oldText entries. And edit 3 has three sub-changes. Let me list all oldText/newText pairs:\n\n1. Constants block: add FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE.\n2. reimbursementRate: `: 1;` → `: FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE;` — but `: 1;` might not be unique. Let me use more context: \n```\n    ? HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_RATE\n    : 1;\n```\nThat's unique.\n3. `let desired = 0;` → `let desiredPayout = 0;` — unique.\n4. `desired += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;` → `desiredPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;` — unique.\n5. `Math.min(desired, policy.remainingCap)` → `Math.min(desiredPayout, policy.remainingCap)` — unique.\n\nLet me do all in one edit call.\n\nThen run tests.\n\nLet me also prepare the APP mass calculation for documentation.\n\nCurrent mass of affected code (reimbursementRate + processClaim):\n\nreimbursementRate:\n- `item: Item`: binding (1)\n- `(item.enchantment ?? 0)`: binding item.enchantment (1) + constant 0 (1) = 2\n- `>= HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD`: binding (1)\n- ternary `?:`: conditional (4)\n- `HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_RATE`: binding (1)\n- `1`: constant (1)\n- subtotal: 1 + 2 + 1 + 4 + 1 + 1 = 10\n\nprocessClaim:\n- `policy: Policy, incident: Incident`: 2 bindings (2)\n- `pool = [...policy.items]`: binding pool (1) + assignment (6) + ... spread/policy.items binding (1) = 8\n- `let desired = 0`: binding desired (1) + constant 0 (1) + assignment (6) = 8\n- `for (const damage of incident.damages)`: loop (5) + binding damage (1) + binding incident.damages (1) = 7\n- `const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);`: binding index (1) + assignment (6) + invocation findIndex (2) + binding it (1) + binding it.type (1) + binding damage.itemType (1) = 12\n- `if (index === -1)`: conditional (4) + binding index (1) + constant -1 (1) = 6\n- `throw new Error(...)`: invocation (2) + template... constant string (1) + binding damage.itemType (1) = 4\n- `const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);`: binding item (1) + assignment (6) + invocation splice (2) + binding pool (1) + binding index (1) + constant 1 (1) = 12\n- `desired += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;`: assignment (6) + invocation reimbursementRate (2) + binding item (1) + binding damage.amount (1) + constant DEDUCTIBLE (1) = 11\n- `const payout = roundDown(Math.min(desired, policy.remainingCap));`: binding payout (1) + assignment (6) + invocation roundDown (2) + invocation Math.min (2) + binding desired (1) + binding policy.remainingCap (1) = 13\n- `policy.remainingCap -= payout;`: assignment (6) + binding policy.remainingCap (1) + binding payout (1) = 8\n- `return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };`: binding payout (1) + binding policy.remainingCap (1) = 2\n- subtotal: 2 + 8 + 8 + 7 + 12 + 6 + 4 + 12 + 11 + 13 + 8 + 2 = 93\n\nThis is getting very detailed and approximate. The key point: my refactoring (rename + constant) changes:\n- `1` (constant, mass 1) → `FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE` (binding, mass 1): no change.\n- `desired` (binding) → `desiredPayout` (binding): no change.\n- Added constant declaration `FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = 1`: binding (1) + constant (1) + assignment (6) = 8.\n\nWait, adding the constant declaration adds mass 8 (a module-level const assignment). Hmm. So mass INCREASES by 8 from adding the constant declaration, while the usage site is unchanged.\n\nThat means my refactoring increases total mass by 8. That's a mass penalty. Per guidelines Rule 2 trumps APP, but adding 8 mass for naming a `1` that's used once... is it worth it?\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. A module-level `const FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE = 1` adds: the name (binding, 1), the literal 1 (constant, 1), and the assignment `=` (6) = 8. And the single use changes from literal `1` (constant, 1) to `FULL_REIMBURSEMENT_RATE` (binding, 1) = no change at use site. Net: +8 mass.\n\nIs naming a single-use magic `1` worth +8 mass? The guideline balance: Rule 2 (clarity) vs Rule 4 (fewest elements) vs APP. Adding a constant for a single-use `1` that's already documented by the function comment \"50% if highly enchanted (>= 8), else full\" — the \"else full\" already explains the `1`. So the constant is somewhat redundant with the comment. And +8 mass + 1 new element.\n\nI think naming the `1` is actually marginal-to-negative when you account for the added declaration. The comment already says \"else full.\" So the