ool.find((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (!item) {\n    throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  pool.splice(pool.indexOf(item), 1);\n  desiredPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\nThis uses `find` (returns the item) + `indexOf` + `splice`. But that's two passes (find then indexOf). The current findIndex+splice is one pass for the index. Current is fine/better. Leave it.\n\nAlternatively:\n```typescript\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (index === -1) {\n    throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  const item = pool[index];\n  pool.splice(index, 1);\n  desiredPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\nThis replaces `const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);` (destructuring the returned array) with `const item = pool[index]; pool.splice(index, 1);`. Is that clearer? The destructuring `const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1)` is a bit clever (splice returns an array of removed items, we take the first). The two-line version is more explicit: get the item, then remove it. But it's two statements vs one. Marginal. I think the destructure is fine and idiomatic. Leave it.\n\nOK. Let me also reconsider: is there genuinely no better refactoring? The code is actually quite clean. The most defensible improvement is the `desired` → `desiredPayout` rename. Let me also reconsider if `desired` is really vague. In context: `let desired = 0;` then `desired += ... - DEDUCTIBLE;` then `Math.min(desired, policy.remainingCap)`. A reader can infer desired = desired payout. But `desiredPayout` makes it explicit and parallels `payout` and `remainingCap` (the ClaimResult fields). I think it's a real, if small, improvement. \n\nGiven the mandate to attempt at least one refactoring, and that the code is already quite clean, I'll do the rename as the primary refactoring and document thoroughly why further refactors (consolidation, helper extraction, constant naming) were considered and rejected (they'd increase mass / add abstractions without clear clarity benefit). This is exactly the \"document why none [further] is possible\" path combined with one real improvement.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether I can find a refactoring that reduces mass. Lowering mass is the strongest signal. \n\nLooking again... The `pool` copy `[...policy.items]` — needed because we splice. Can't avoid.\n\n`Object.values(componentCounts)` in policyBasePremium — needed.\n\nHmm, what about `reimbursementRate`'s `(item.enchantment ?? 0)` — the `?? 0` defaults enchantment to 0. This pattern appears in `itemSurcharges` too: `(item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD`. Extracting `itemEnchantment(item): number = item.enchantment ?? 0` would dedupe. Let me compute mass:\n\nBefore (two uses):\n- In reimbursementRate: `(item.enchantment ?? 0)` → binding item.enchantment (1) + constant 0 (1) = 2\n- In itemSurcharges: `(item.enchantment ?? 0)` → 2\n- total: 4\n\nAfter (helper + two uses):\n- Helper `const itemEnchantment = (item: Item) => item.enchantment ?? 0;`: binding item (1) + binding item.enchantment (1) + constant 0 (1) = 3 (plus the const declaration assignment 6 → 9? hmm)\n- Use in reimbursementRate: `itemEnchantment(item)` invocation (2) + binding item (1) = 3\n- Use in itemSurcharges: `itemEnchantment(item)` = 3\n- total: 9 (helper, with assignment) + 3 + 3 = 15. Or if helper doesn't count assignment... it's still 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 > 4.\n\nSo extracting `itemEnchantment` increases mass (from 4 to ~9-15). Not worth it. The `?? 0` is a tiny idiom. Skip.\n\nOK. I'm confident the code is already quite clean and the only clearly-beneficial, zero-risk, zero-mass-penalty improvement is the `desired` → `desiredPayout` rename. Let me also reconsider one more: the comment on processClaim says \"mutating its remaining cap\" —