harges`:\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += CURSE_RATE * base;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) surcharge += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base;\n  return surcharge;\n}\n```\nCould rewrite as:\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  return (\n    (item.cursed ? CURSE_RATE * base : 0) +\n    ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD ? HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base : 0)\n  );\n}\n```\nMass: before has two `if` (2 conditionals = 8) + two `surcharge +=` assignments (12) + `let surcharge = 0` (8) + return binding (1) = ... after has two ternaries (8) + two `* base` (bindings) + `+` + return invocation... roughly similar, maybe slightly less (removes `let`/`+=` assignments). But this is not part of the recently-added code and isn't clearly better (the if/if is very readable). Also \"Never refactor multiple things at once\" — I should focus. Skip.\n\nOK, committing: rename `desired` → `desiredPayout`. Let me do it.\n\nActually, I realize I should make the refactoring a bit more meaningful to honor the spirit. Let me reconsider combining the rename with extracting `reimbursementFor` — accepting the +6 mass — IF I judge the clarity gain real. \n\nLet me honestly assess: does `desiredPayout += reimbursementFor(item, damage.amount)` read better than `desiredPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE`?\n\nThe inline formula `reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE` is a single, scannable arithmetic expression. A reader sees: rate × amount − deductible. That's the actual business rule, visible. Extracting hides it behind a name AND requires a jump. For a one-line formula, inline is generally preferred (Rule 4: fewest elements; and clarity — the formula IS the intent). So extraction would HURT clarity here. Confirmed: don't extract.\n\nSo the single b