Premium(items)` - \"Summed base premium of all quoted items, applying the 3-alike-component block.\" This computes the base premium for items before surcharges/discounts. The name is reasonable. Actually, looking more carefully, it returns the base premium sum including the block discount logic. It's accurate enough.\n\n2. `itemBaseValue(item)` - \"Per-item base value: a single item's premium, before block discounts.\" Good.\n\n3. `itemInsuranceValue(item)` - \"Per-item insurance value.\" Good.\n\n4. `insuranceSum(items)` - \"Total insured value of a list of items (cap = CAP_MULTIPLIER * this).\" Good.\n\n5. `itemSurcharges(item)` - \"Item-specific risk surcharges (cursed, high enchantment).\" Good.\n\n6. `reimbursementRate(item)` - \"Reimbursement rate for a damaged item: 50% if highly enchanted (>= 8), else full.\" Good.\n\n7. `processClaim(policy, incident)` - \"Process a claim against a policy, mutating its remaining cap.\" Good.\n\n8. `quotePremium(customer, items, isFollowUp)` - \"Total premium for a quote.\" Good.\n\n9. `runScenario(input)` - main entry. Good.\n\nNow let me look more carefully for potential issues.\n\nLooking at `policyBasePremium`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis is a bit complex. It separates components from non-components, then applies block logic to components. The block logic only applies a discount when `count === BLOCK_SIZE` exactly (3). Let me check the tests:\n- 2 runes -> premium 60. So 2 components: 2*25 = 50 base, +10% first insurance = 5, +5 fee = 60. ✓\n- 3 runes -> premium 71. So block: 60 base, +10% = 6, +5 fee = 71. ✓\n- 4 runes -> premium 115. So 4 components, no block (count !== 3): 4*25 = 100, +10% = 10, +5 = 115. ✓\n- 7 runes -> premium 198. 7 components, count=7 !== 3: 7*25 = 175, +10% = 17.5, ceil = 18, +5 = 198. ✓\n- 2 runes + 1 moonstone -> premium 88. runes count=2, moonstone count=1. 2*25 + 1*25 = 75, +10% = 7.5, ceil = 8, +5 = 88. ✓\n- 3 runes + 3 moonstones -> premium 137. rune block 60 + moonstone block 60 = 120, +10% = 12, +5 = 137. ✓\n\nOK so the logic is correct. The block applies only when count is exactly 3.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. The block discount applies to a \"block of 3 alike.\" So for 6 runes, would it be two blocks? Let me check: the test only has 7 runes -> premium 198 (no block applied since count=7 !== 3). So the current implementation only applies block discount when count is exactly 3. That matches the tests.\n\nActually wait, this seems like a potential simplification. The current logic is: if count === 3, use BLOCK_PREMIUM (60), else use count * COMPONENT_BASE (25 each). \n\nBut actually, looking at the spec comment: \"a block of 3 alike is offered cheaper.\" This is the design. The implementation matches the tests. I shouldn't change the logic.\n\nLet me look at the structure more. There's some duplication or conceptual overlap:\n\nIn `policyBasePremium`, components are handled separately. `itemBaseValue` returns `COMPONENT_BASE` for components. But in `policyBasePremium`, components are NOT added via `itemBaseValue` in the loop — they're counted and added later. So `itemBaseValue` for components is only used in `itemSurcharges` (cursed/high-enchant surcharges use `itemBaseValue`).\n\nWait, let me check `itemSurcharges`:\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += CURSE_RATE * base;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) surcharge += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base;\n  return surcharge;\n}\n```\n\nSo surcharges use `itemBaseValue(item)` as the base. For a component (rune/moonstone), `itemBaseValue` returns `COMPONENT_BASE = 25`. So a cursed rune would have a curse surcharge of 0.5 * 25 = 12.5. There's no test for cursed components, so I can't verify, but the logic is consistent.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtle conceptual issue: `itemBaseValue` for a component returns `COMPONENT_BASE` (25), but the actual base premium contributed by a component in a block of 3 is `BLOCK_PREMIUM / 3 = 20`. So the surcharge base differs from the actual base premium when a block applies. But again, no test covers this, so I should leave it.\n\nNow, let me think about the actual refactoring opportunities.\n\n### Rule 2: Reveals Intent\n\nThe code is quite well-documented with good names and comments. Let me look for clarity improvements.\n\nLooking at `processClaim`:\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nThis looks clean. The `pool` variable is a copy of policy items so each damage consumes one item. Good naming.\n\n### Rule 3: No Duplication\n\nLet me look for duplication.\n\n`itemBaseValue` and `itemInsuranceValue` are structurally similar:\n```typescript\nfunction itemBaseValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_BASE : BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n\nfunction itemInsuranceValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_INSURANCE_VALUE : INSURANCE_VALUE[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n```\n\nThese could be unified with a lookup-table approach, but they're conceptually different (premium vs insurance value) and the duplication is minimal. Combining might hurt clarity. I'll consider this.\n\nActually, there's a deeper structural observation. Both `BASE_PREMIUM` and `INSURANCE_VALUE` map the same item types to values, and `INSURANCE_VALUE = 10 * BASE_PREMIUM` for all main items! Let me check:\n- sword: 100 -> 1000 ✓\n- amulet: 60 -> 600 ✓\n- staff: 80 -> 800 ✓\n- potion: 40 -> 400 ✓\n\nSo `INSURANCE_VALUE` is exactly `BASE_PREMIUM * 10` for all main items. And `COMPONENT_INSURANCE_VALUE = 250 = COMPONENT_BASE * 10` (25 * 10 = 250). \n\nThis is a real conceptual duplication! The insurance value is always 10x the base premium. This is a single representation opportunity (DRY). If we derive insurance value from base premium, we eliminate the duplicate `INSURANCE_VALUE` table and `COMPONENT_INSURANCE_VALUE` constant.\n\nBut wait — is this a coincidence of the test data, or an actual domain rule? Looking at the numbers, it's a consistent 10x relationship across all items. The domain spec might state \"insurance value is 10x the base premium.\" But I have to be careful: I only have the tests. The tests all pass with the 10x relationship. If I refactor to derive insurance value from base premium, all tests still pass (since the relationship holds for all tested items).\n\nHowever, this is risky. If the relationship is coincidental and a future test uses an item where insurance value ≠ 10x base premium, this refactoring would break. But per TDD/refactoring principles, I refactor based on current tests. The relationship holds for ALL items currently defined. \n\nHmm, but actually, let me reconsider. The spec mentions \"Insurance value per single main item type; components are 250 G each.\" and \"Base premium per single main item type.\" These are described as separate concepts. The 10x might be coincidental to the chosen numbers.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether this is a safe refactoring. The risk: deriving insurance value as `10 * itemBaseValue` would conflate two concepts that the spec treats as separate. This could hurt Rule 2 (reveals intent) — the code would no longer clearly show that insurance values are a separate concept. \n\nPer the rules: \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass\" and \"Balance with Rule 2: If DRY hurts clarity, choose clarity.\"\n\nI think deriving insurance from premium could hurt clarity because the two are conceptually distinct domain quantities (a premium is what you pay; insurance value is what's covered). Collapsing them hides the domain distinction. So I'll be cautious here. This is a judgment call.\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. The whole point of DRY is \"knowledge should have single representation.\" If the insurance value truly is always 10x the base premium (a domain rule), then representing it twice is duplication. If it's coincidental, it's not duplication — it's two independent facts that happen to align.\n\nGiven I only have tests, and the tests don't establish the 10x as a rule (they just have values that happen to be 10x), I should NOT assume it's a rule. Consolidating would be speculative. So I'll leave the two tables separate. This is the conservative, correct call — don't introduce coupling based on unverified assumptions.\n\nOK so let me look for safer, clearer refactoring opportunities.\n\n### Looking at `runScenario`\n\n```typescript\nexport function runScenario(input: unknown): { results: StepResult[] } {\n  const scenario = input as Scenario;\n  const results: StepResult[] = [];\n  const policies = new Map<number, Policy>();\n  let hasPriorQuote = false;\n  scenario.steps.forEach((step, index) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const items = step.items ?? [];\n      results.push({ premium: quotePremium(scenario.customer, items, hasPriorQuote) });\n      hasPriorQuote = true;\n      policies.set(index, { items, remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(items) });\n    } else {\n      const policy = policies.get(step.policy);\n      if (!policy) throw new Error(`claim references unknown policy: ${step.policy}`);\n      results.push(processClaim(policy, step.incident));\n    }\n  });\n  return { results };\n}\n```\n\nThis uses `scenario.steps.forEach((step, index) => ...)`. There's a subtle thing: `policies.set(index, ...)` uses the step index as the policy key, and claims reference `step.policy` (which is an index into steps). This works because the test always uses `policy: 0` referring to the first step.\n\nThe `forEach` with mutation of external state (`results.push`, `hasPriorQuote = true`, `policies.set`) is a bit imperative. Could be a `for...of` loop with an index. Actually, using `forEach` here means we can't easily use the index without the second callback param, which is fine. But `for...of` with `entries()` might be cleaner. This is minor.\n\nActually, there's a more meaningful observation: the `hasPriorQuote` flag and the `policies` map and `results` are all mutated inside the forEach. This is standard imperative processing. It's clear enough.\n\nLet me look at the magic of `quotePremium`:\n\n```typescript\nfunction quotePremium(\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  items: Item[],\n  isFollowUp: boolean,\n): number {\n  const policyBase = policyBasePremium(items);\n  const surcharges = items.reduce((sum, it) => sum + itemSurcharges(it), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  return roundUp(\n    policyBase + surcharges + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThis is clean and readable. Each line is a named calculation. Good.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider the whole thing. The code is actually quite clean already. Let me think about what genuine improvement I can make.\n\n### Potential Refactoring: `policyBasePremium` simplification\n\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis has a `for...of` with conditional branching and a separate loop for component counts. The component count accumulation `(componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1` is a common pattern.\n\nOne option: extract a helper for the component block cost. But it's only used once. Per Rule 4 (fewest elements), extracting a one-use helper adds an element. Not beneficial unless it improves clarity significantly.\n\nActually, the block-cost calculation `count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE` is a single ternary — clear enough inline.\n\n### Looking at the constants organization\n\nThe constants are grouped with comments. The structure is fine.\n\n### Let me reconsider the `forEach` vs `for...of` in runScenario\n\n`scenario.steps.forEach((step, index) => { ... })` — the callback mutates `results`, `hasPriorQuote`, `policies`. This works but `forEach` with side effects is slightly less clear than a `for` loop. Also, `forEach` can't be `await`ed, but there's no async here. Converting to `for...of` with `.entries()`:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const [index, step] of scenario.steps.entries()) {\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThis is marginally cleaner and avoids the closure. But it's a wash on mass. Hmm. Actually, this doesn't clearly improve things. Let me not do a churn-only change.\n\n### Let me look for actual duplication more carefully\n\n`itemBaseValue` and `itemInsuranceValue`:\n```typescript\nfunction itemBaseValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_BASE : BASE_PREMIUM[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n\nfunction itemInsuranceValue(item: Item): number {\n  return isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_INSURANCE_VALUE : INSURANCE_VALUE[item.type] ?? 0;\n}\n```\n\nAs discussed, the 10x relationship. I'll leave these separate due to the conceptual-distinctness concern.\n\n### Re-examine: is there dead code or unused elements?\n\nLet me check exports. `runScenario` is exported and used by cli and tests. `QuoteResult`, `ClaimResult`, `StepResult` are exported types. Are they used externally? The test imports `runScenario` only. The types are exported but maybe unused. Let me check.\n\nActually `StepResult` is the return type's element. Exporting types is fine for API. Not dead code per se.\n\n### Let me reconsider naming once more (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\nThe instructions emphasize naming as the first priority. Let me scrutinize each name against its actual purpose as revealed by tests:\n\n1. `policyBasePremium` — computes the sum of base premiums (with block discount for components). The name says \"policy base premium.\" Actually it returns the *summed* base premium of items, which becomes the `policyBase` in `quotePremium`. The name is accurate. Could be `sumBasePremium` but `policyBasePremium` is fine and arguably clearer (it's the base premium for the policy/quote). Keep.\n\n2. `itemBaseValue` — returns the base premium of a single item (component = 25, main = lookup). \"value\" is slightly vague; could be `itemBasePremium`. Hmm. The comment says \"Per-item base value: a single item's premium, before block discounts.\" So it IS the premium. Calling it `itemBaseValue` vs `itemBasePremium`... The function is used in two contexts: summing premiums (in policyBasePremium) and as the surcharge base (in itemSurcharges). In the surcharge context, \"value\" might be more apt than \"premium\" since it's the base for calculating surcharges. Actually it represents the item's base premium either way. I think `itemBaseValue` is acceptable. Renaming to `itemBasePremium` would be marginally more precise. But this is borderline churn.\n\nLet me think about whether any name genuinely doesn't fit:\n\n- `insuranceSum` — sums insurance values. Accurate.\n- `reimbursementRate` — returns 1 or 0.5. \"Rate\" is accurate. \n- `processClaim` — accurate.\n- `quotePremium` — accurate.\n- `itemSurcharges` — returns the surcharge amount for an item. \"Surcharges\" (plural) — it could be curse + high-enchant. Accurate. Could be `itemSurcharge` (singular, the total). Minor.\n- `isComponent` — accurate.\n\nHmm, `itemSurcharges` returns a single number (the total surcharge for the item), despite being potentially composed of two surcharges. The plural \"surcharges\" suggests multiple, but it returns one combined value. Singular `itemSurcharge` might be more accurate. But this is very minor and the plural arguably reflects that it aggregates multiple surcharge sources. I'll leave it.\n\n### Let me look at `cli.ts` for refactoring opportunities\n\n```typescript\n#!/usr/bin/env node\nimport { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\nimport { runScenario } from \"./office.js\";\n\nfunction main(): void {\n  const input = readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\");\n  const scenario = JSON.parse(input);\n  const result = runScenario(scenario);\n  process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(result));\n}\n\ntry {\n  main();\n} catch (err) {\n  const message = err instanceof Error ? err.message : String(err);\n  process.stderr.write(`${message}\\n`);\n  process.exit(1);\n}\n```\n\nThis is clean and minimal. `main()` reads, parses, runs, writes. The try/catch handles errors. \n\nOne observation: `const scenario = JSON.parse(input);` then `runScenario(scenario)`. The intermediate `scenario` variable just passes through. Could inline: `const result = runScenario(JSON.parse(input));`. This removes a binding (Rule 4, lower mass). But the named variable `scenario` adds a tiny bit of clarity. It's a wash. Actually, inlining reduces a binding and an assignment. Per Rule 4 (fewest elements) and APP mass, inlining is slightly better, and the clarity loss is negligible since `JSON.parse(input)` clearly produces a scenario. Hmm, but `runScenario` already names it. I think this is genuinely marginal.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The `main` function does three things: read, parse+run, write. The `scenario` variable is only used once. Inlining `JSON.parse(input)` into `runScenario(...)` is a reasonable simplification. But honestly the current form is perfectly clear. I don't want to make churn changes.\n\nLet me focus on finding ONE genuine, defensible improvement.\n\n### Reconsidering the insurance-value / base-premium relationship\n\nLet me reconsider. I'll re-examine whether collapsing `INSURANCE_VALUE` into a derivation from `BASE_PREMIUM` is justified.\n\nThe domain: \"Base premium per single main item type\" and \"Insurance value per single main item type; components are 250 G each.\" These are presented as two separate tables in the spec. The fact that insurance = 10 × premium for every entry is either (a) a domain rule, or (b) coincidence of chosen numbers.\n\nTDD refactoring principle: refactor based on what tests establish. The tests establish specific input→output mappings. They do NOT establish \"insurance = 10 × premium\" as a rule. If I collapse them and a later test adds an item where insurance ≠ 10× premium, I'd break. But refactoring is about the *current* code+tests. \n\nHowever, the safer interpretation: these are two independent domain concepts. Even if numerically related today, conceptually they're separate (what you pay vs. what's covered). DRY says \"knowledge should have single representation\" — but here the *knowledge* is \"sword's insurance value is 1000\" and \"sword's base premium is 100.\" These are two pieces of knowledge that happen to relate. Consolidating them assumes a relationship the spec/tests don't confirm.\n\nGiven the risk and the Rule 2 concern (collapsing distinct domain concepts hurts clarity), I'll NOT do this refactoring. This is the disciplined choice.\n\nSo what CAN I refactor?\n\nLet me re-examine more carefully for genuine improvements.\n\n### Examining `policyBasePremium` — the two-loop structure\n\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis splits items into components (counted) and non-components (summed immediately), then applies block pricing to component counts. \n\nIs there duplication? The block-pricing logic `count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE` — note that `BLOCK_PREMIUM = 60` and `BLOCK_SIZE * COMPONENT_BASE = 3 * 25 = 75`, so the block saves 15. The logic says \"if exactly 3, charge 60; else charge 25 each.\"\n\nThis is the only place block logic appears. No duplication.\n\nCould the two loops be merged? Not easily, because block pricing needs the total count per component type, which requires accumulating first. The two-pass approach is necessary.\n\nHmm, this is fine.\n\n### Let me look at the rounding helpers\n\n```typescript\nconst EPSILON = 1e-9;\nconst roundUp = (amount: number): number => Math.ceil(amount - EPSILON);\nconst roundDown = (amount: number): number => Math.floor(amount + EPSILON);\n```\n\nThese are clean, well-commented. Used in `quotePremium` (roundUp