tests green. The 10× relationship is empirically universal in the current test suite. \n\nBUT — and this is key — the spec (as reflected in comments) treats them as conceptually separate: \"Base premium per single main item type\" vs \"Insurance value per single main item type.\" The spec author chose to specify them separately. This is evidence that they're meant to be independent concepts, even if the numbers happen to align. Refactoring to derive one from the other would be imposing an inferred relationship that the spec doesn't state.\n\nGiven Rule 2 (reveals intent) is higher priority than Rule 3 (DRY), and the spec's intent is clearly to treat these as separate concepts, I should NOT collapse them. The current separate representation better reveals the domain intent. This is the correct, defensible call.\n\nSo, decision: KEEP the separate tables. Document why.\n\n### So what refactoring DO I make?\n\nI must attempt at least one. Let me find the best candidate.\n\nLet me re-scan for:\n- Unclear names (Rule 2)\n- Real duplication (Rule 3)\n- Unnecessary elements (Rule 4)\n\n#### Candidate: `itemSurcharges` ternary-free? No.\n\n#### Candidate: `runScenario` `forEach` → `for...of`\n\nLet me reconsider this seriously. The `forEach` callback:\n- Captures `results`, `hasPriorQuote`, `policies` via closure.\n- Mutates all of them.\n- Has early `throw` (which works in forEach since it propagates).\n\nUsing `for...of` with index:\n```typescript\nfor (let index = 0; index < scenario.steps.length; index++) {\n  const step = scenario.steps[index];\n  ...\n}\n```\nor\n```typescript\nfor (const [index, step] of scenario.steps.entries()) {\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nThe `for...of` version is more idiomatic for imperative side-effectful loops and avoids the closure. But as computed, mass is roughly neutral.\n\nHmm, actually, I realize there might be a cleaner approach. The `index` is only used as the policy key (`policies.set(index, ...)`). And claims reference `step.policy` which is the step index. So the index IS the policy id. This is a slightly fragile coupling (policy id = step position), but it works and matches the spec (claims reference a policy by its step index).\n\n#### Candidate: extract `Policy` creation into a helper?\n\n```typescript\npolicies.set(index, { items, remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(items) });\n```\nvs\n```typescript\npolicies.set(index, createPolicy(items));\n```\nwhere `createPolicy(items) = { items, remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(items) }`.\n\nThis extracts a concept (\"create a policy from quoted items\") which improves Rule 2 (reveals intent — the policy creation logic is named). It's used once, so Rule 4 (fewest elements) slightly disfavors. But Rule 2 > Rule 4. The cap calculation `CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(items)` is a piece of domain logic that's currently inline; naming it `createPolicy` or `policyCap` reveals intent better.\n\nHmm, but it's a one-liner used once. Extracting might be over-engineering. The comment on the `Policy` type already explains \"remaining payout cap.\"\n\nActually, you know what, let me reconsider the whole `runScenario` for clarity. The quote branch does three things:\n1. Compute premium (with follow-up flag).\n2. Push premium result.\n3. Set follow-up flag.\n4. Store policy with cap.\n\nThe ordering matters: `quotePremium` uses `hasPriorQuote` (before it's updated), then `hasPriorQuote = true`. This is correct.\n\nI think `runScenario` is reasonably clear. \n\n#### Let me reconsider `itemBaseValue` naming.\n\n`itemBaseValue` → the comment says \"a single item's premium, before block discounts.\" So it IS a premium. The name \"value\" is vague. Renaming to `itemBasePremium` would better reveal intent (Rule 2, first priority). \n\nBut wait — is it really always a \"premium\"? It's used as:\n1. The base premium contribution (in `policyBasePremium`, for non-components).\n2. The base for surcharge calculation (in `itemSurcharges`).\n\nIn both cases, it represents the item's base premium. So `itemBasePremium` is more accurate than `itemBaseValue`. This is a legitimate Rule 2 naming improvement.\n\nHmm, but \"value\" might be intentionally generic because it's the per-item value that feeds both premium AND surcharge base. Actually no — it's specifically the base premium. The insurance value is separate (`itemInsuranceValue`). So `itemBaseValue` is ambiguous: base *what* value? Premium or insurance? The sibling `itemInsuranceValue` is specific. For symmetry and clarity, `itemBasePremium` would be clearer.\n\nWait, but there's `itemInsuranceValue` (insurance) and `itemBaseValue` (premium). If I rename to `itemBasePremium`, then we have `itemBasePremium` and `itemInsuranceValue` — both specific. Good. This improves Rule 2.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider. Is `itemBaseValue` really the premium? For components, it returns `COMPONENT_BASE = 25`. The component's premium contribution in a block is 20 (60/3), or 25 standalone. So `itemBaseValue` for a component returns 25, which is the *standalone* (non-block) base premium. So yes, it's the per-item base premium before block discount. `itemBasePremium` is accurate.\n\nBut does renaming break anything? It's an internal function (not exported). Renaming is safe. Let me verify it's only used internally.\n\nUsed in: `policyBasePremium`, `itemSurcharges`. Both in same file. Safe to rename.\n\nThis is a clean Rule 2 improvement: `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium`, with comment update.\n\nBut is this \"enough\" of a refactoring? It's a naming improvement, which the instructions explicitly say is the FIRST priority. Yes, this counts.\n\nBut let me see if I can do something more impactful too. Actually, the instructions say \"Make ONE improvement at a time\" and \"Run tests after each change.\" But also the subagent should \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring.\" I can do the rename and verify, that's one solid improvement.\n\nActually, let me reconsider whether to also address a small duplication or element count. Let me look once more...\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider the `INSURANCE_VALUE` / `BASE_PREMIUM` 10× thing from a different angle. Instead of collapsing them (which I decided against for Rule 2 reasons), is there a different DRY improvement?\n\nWhat about the component special-casing? `itemBaseValue` and `itemInsuranceValue` both have `isComponent(item.type) ? COMPONENT_X : TABLE[item.type] ?? 0`. This is structural duplication. Both functions branch on `isComponent` and return either a component constant or a table lookup. \n\nCould I unify with a single lookup that returns `{premium, insurance}` per item type? That's a bigger refactor. The current approach has 2 functions × 1 ternary each. A unified approach might be a single `itemValue(item, \"premium\"|\"insurance\")` or a config table. But these add complexity. Per Rule 4, the current 2 small functions are fine. The \"duplication\" is just the `isComponent` check, which is minimal. Not worth consolidating.\n\nOK. Let me also reconsider: is there genuinely nothing better than a rename?\n\nLet me look at `cli.ts` once more. The `main` function:\n```typescript\nfunction main(): void {\n  const input = readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\");\n  const scenario = JSON.parse(input);\n  const result = runScenario(scenario);\n  process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(result));\n}\n```\n\nThe `scenario` variable is a pure pass-through. Inlining: `const result = runScenario(JSON.parse(input));`. This removes one binding + one assignment. But `scenario` adds a tiny bit of self-documentation. Trade-off. I think inlining is fine and slightly better (fewer elements, Rule 4). But it's marginal.\n\nHmm, actually, I think the cleaner refactoring in cli.ts is to keep `main` but the structure is fine. Let me not churn cli.ts.\n\nLet me reconsider `office.ts` holistically for the single best improvement.\n\nActually, I just realized something about `policyBasePremium`. Let me re-read:\n\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBaseValue(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nWait, the second loop iterates `Object.values(componentCounts)`. For each component TYPE, it gets the count and adds the block-adjusted cost. So if there are 3 runes and 3 moonstones, componentCounts = {rune: 3, moonstone: 3}, and it adds BLOCK_PREMIUM (60) twice = 120. ✓ (test: 3 runes + 3 moonstones -> premium 137 = 120 + 12 + 5 ✓).\n\nThis is correct. The logic: each component type's count is checked for the block (exactly 3 → 60, else 25×count).\n\nOK this is fine. No change needed here.\n\n### Decision\n\nI'll do the naming refactoring: `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium` (Rule 2, first priority), updating the comment. This is a clean, defensible, intent-revealing improvement.\n\nBut wait — is that too trivial? The instructions say \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring.\" A rename IS a valid refactoring, and naming is explicitly the first priority. But let me see if there's a slightly more substantial one I can combine.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the `forEach` → `for...of` in `runScenario`. I keep dismissing it as mass-neutral, but let me actually compute carefully.\n\nCurrent `runScenario` body (the forEach):\n```typescript\nscenario.steps.forEach((step, index) => {\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    const items = step.items ?? [];\n    results.push({ premium: quotePremium(scenario.customer, items, hasPriorQuote) });\n    hasPriorQuote = true;\n    policies.set(index, { items, remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(items) });\n  } else {\n    const policy = policies.get(step.policy);\n    if (!policy) throw new Error(`claim references unknown policy: ${step.policy}`);\n    results.push(processClaim(policy, step.incident));\n  }\n});\n```\n\nMass of the forEach construct itself:\n- `scenario.steps` : scenario (binding, 1), steps (binding access, 0? property access — APP doesn't list property access as a separate category; treat as part of invocation/binding). Let me count `scenario.steps.forEach(...)`:\n  - `forEach` is an invocation (2) and a loop (5) = 7.\n  - The arrow function `(step, index) => {...}` — step, index are bindings (2).\n- Inside callback: `if` (4) ×2 (two ifs: `step.op === \"quote\"` and `!policy`), etc.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me not over-analyze mass. \n\nThe cleaner argument for `for...of`: `forEach` with side effects is a mild code smell; a `for...of` loop more honestly signals imperative mutation. This is a Rule 2 (reveals intent) argument. Combined with the rename, these are two separate improvements though.\n\nThe instruction says \"Make ONE improvement at a time. Run tests after each change.\" So I should do one, test, then optionally another. But as the refactor subagent, I think doing a focused set is fine as long as I test after. Actually, let me be disciplined: do the rename first (highest priority, Rule 2), test, then evaluate if another improvement is warranted.\n\nHmm, but actually, re-reading: \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring.\" One is enough. But I should aim for genuine value.\n\nLet me reconsider if the `forEach` → `for...of` is worth it. Actually, I think the cleanest single improvement that genuinely helps is the rename `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium`. Let me also consider whether `itemSurcharges` should be `itemSurcharge` (singular). \n\n`itemSurcharges(item)` returns a single number: the total surcharge for that item. The plural \"surcharges\" is because it may include multiple (curse + high-enchant). But it returns ONE combined value. Compare: `policyBasePremium` (singular premium). For consistency and accuracy, `itemSurcharge` (singular — the item's total surcharge) might be better. But \"surcharges\" arguably conveys \"the sum of applicable surcharges.\" This is genuinely ambiguous. I'll leave `itemSurcharges` since the plural communicates that multiple surcharge types may apply. Not a clear win to change.\n\nOK, let me also double-check: is `itemBaseValue` exported? No. Is `BASE_PREMIUM` exported? No. Good, internal rename is safe.\n\nLet me also reconsider the comment. Current comment:\n```typescript\n/** Per-item base value: a single item's premium, before block discounts. */\nfunction itemBaseValue(item: Item): number {\n```\nAfter rename:\n```typescript\n/** Per-item base premium before block discounts. */\nfunction itemBasePremium(item: Item): number {\n```\nThe comment can be tightened since the name now carries \"premium.\"\n\nLet me now also compute APP mass before and after for this rename. Renaming doesn't change mass (names don't affect mass — only structure does). So mass is unchanged. That's fine — Rule 2 (clarity) trumps APP, and a rename is a Rule 2 improvement that's mass-neutral. The instructions explicitly allow this: \"Rule 2 trumps APP: Clarity over low mass.\"\n\nBut the instructions also want me to \"Calculate APP mass before and after.\" For a pure rename, mass is identical. I'll document that.\n\nHmm, but the instructions emphasize \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring\" and to calculate mass before/after. A mass-neutral rename is a valid refactoring (improves Rule 2), but let me see if I can ALSO find a mass-reducing improvement to make the refactoring more substantive.\n\nLet me look ONE more time for a mass-reducing opportunity...\n\n#### `cli.ts` `scenario` inlining (mass-reducing)\n\n```typescript\nconst input = readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\");\nconst scenario = JSON.parse(input);\nconst result = runScenario(scenario);\n```\n→\n```typescript\nconst input = readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\");\nconst result = runScenario(JSON.parse(input));\n```\n\nMass change: removes `scenario` binding (1) and the assignment `scenario = JSON.parse(input)` (assignment, 6). Wait — `const scenario = JSON.parse(input)` — is `const x = ...` an assignment (6) or a binding (1)? APP: \"Binding/Scalar (Mass: 1): Variables, parameters\" and \"Assignment (Mass: 6): Mutations (x = 5, count++)\". A `const` declaration is a binding initialization. Is it an \"assignment\"? The rule says \"Mutations.\" A `const` init is arguably not a mutation (it's initialization, not reassignment). This is ambiguous. \n\nIf `const scenario = ...` counts as binding (1) only, inlining removes 1 mass. If it counts as assignment (6), inlining removes 6. Either way, inlining reduces mass and removes an element (Rule 4). And clarity is preserved (the variable was a pure pass-through).\n\nBut does inlining hurt clarity? `runScenario(JSON.parse(input))` — reads stdin, parses JSON, runs scenario. Still clear. The `scenario` named variable was mildly self-documenting but `runScenario` already conveys \"scenario.\" I think inlining is a slight Rule 4 + mass improvement without hurting Rule 2.\n\nHmm, but this is cli.ts, which the task says is \"the thin CLI wrapper\" and \"src/office.ts holds all logic.\" The refactor focus should probably be on office.ts (the logic). But cli.ts is fair game too.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The task says \"Note: src/office.ts holds all logic; src/cli.ts is the thin CLI wrapper.\" This is just describing the architecture, not restricting refactoring scope. I can refactor either.\n\nLet me reconsider whether the cli.ts inlining is worth it. The `main` function reads cleanly as a 3-step pipeline. Inlining `scenario` makes it a 2-step pipeline (read, then parse+run, then write). Honestly both are fine. The named `scenario` variable is slightly more readable for a 3-step process. I'm torn.\n\nYou know what, let me make a cleaner decision. Let me focus on the office.ts rename as the primary refactoring (Rule 2, first priority, clearly defensible), AND also do the cli.ts inlining as a secondary mass-reduction (Rule 4). Two small focused improvements, each tested.\n\nWait, but \"Make ONE improvement at a time. Run tests after each change.\" I can do them sequentially: rename → test → inline → test. That respects the rule.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the cli.ts inlining once more. I want to avoid churn. Is the `scenario` variable truly unnecessary? \n\n```typescript\nfunction main(): void {\n  const input = readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\");\n  const scenario = JSON.parse(input);\n  const result = runScenario(scenario);\n  process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(result));\n}\n```\n\nThe three named locals (`input`, `scenario`, `result`) form a clear read→parse→run→write pipeline. Each step is named. This is actually quite readable. Inlining `scenario` gives:\n\n```typescript\nfunction main(): void {\n  const input = readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\");\n  const result = runScenario(JSON.parse(input));\n  process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(result));\n}\n```\n\nNow `input` is used once (in JSON.parse), and `result` is used once (in write). By the same logic, I could inline `input` and `result` too:\n```typescript\nfunction main(): void {\n  process.stdout.write(JSON.stringify(runScenario(JSON.parse(readFileSync(0, \"utf-8\")))));\n}\n```\nBut that's a readability disaster. So clearly some intermediate variables aid clarity. The question is whether `scenario` specifically aids clarity. Since `runScenario` is named for its input, `runScenario(JSON.parse(input))` is clear. I think inlining `scenario` is acceptable but marginal.\n\nHmm, I think I'm overthinking the cli.ts change. Let me NOT inline — it's churn-y and the current form is clean and readable. The named variables form a clear pipeline.\n\n### Final decision\n\nLet me reconsider the whole picture. The code is genuinely clean. The instructions require at least one refactoring attempt. The most defensible, clearly-beneficial improvement is the **naming** improvement: `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium`. This is Rule 2 (first priority), mass-neutral, and makes the name match its documented purpose (\"a single item's premium\").\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider if there's a MORE impactful naming or structural issue I'm missing. Let me re-read the whole office.ts with fresh eyes...\n\n...Re-reading...\n\n`type Item = { type: string; material?: string; enchantment?: number; cursed?: boolean };` — fine.\n\n`reimbursementRate`:\n```typescript\nfunction reimbursementRate(item: Item): number {\n  return (item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_THRESHOLD\n    ? HIGH_ENCHANT_CLAIM_RATE\n    : 1;\n}\n```\nReturns 1 (full) or 0.5 (high-enchant reduced). Name \"rate\" is accurate. Fine.\n\n`processClaim`:\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\n`uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;` — note operator precedence: `*` binds tighter than `-`, so this is `(reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE`. Correct. But mixing `+=` with a `-` in the expression is slightly subtle. Could add parens for clarity: `+= (reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount) - DEDUCTIBLE`. Minor Rule 2 improvement. Hmm, very minor.\n\nActually, there's a subtle issue: `uncappedPayout` accumulates `rate * amount - DEDUCTIBLE` per damage. So if amount is small, a damage could contribute negatively. E.g., amount 50, rate 1: 50 - 100 = -50. Is that intended? The deductible is per-event (per damage). The test \"deductible per event sword+amulet 500+300 -> payout 600\": (500-100) + (300-100) = 400 + 200 = 600. ✓. So yes, per-damage deductible. If a damage's amount < deductible, it'd be negative, reducing the total. No test covers that. Leave it (matches spec: deductible per event).\n\nOK the claim logic is correct and clear.\n\nLet me also reconsider: `pool.splice(index, 1)` removes the matched item so the same item can't be claimed twice. `const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1)` destructures the removed element. Clean.\n\n`roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap))` — caps the payout, then rounds down. Correct.\n\n`policy.remainingCap -= payout;` — mutates policy. Correct (multiple claims against same policy deplete the cap; test \"cap exhaustion two claims\" verifies).\n\nOK. The code is solid.\n\n### Let me reconsider one structural thing: the `runScenario` `forEach` + closure\n\nI'll make a final call: I'll convert `forEach` to `for...of` with `entries()`. Rationale (Rule 2): `forEach` semantically suggests \"apply a pure function to each element,\" but here the callback exists purely for imperative side effects (pushing to results, mutating flags and maps). A `for...of` loop honestly signals \"imperative iteration with mutation.\" This better reveals intent. It also removes the closure capture, making data flow more explicit.\n\nMass: `forEach` = invocation(2) + loop(5) = 7. `for...of` with `entries()` = loop(5) + entries invocation(2) = 7. Mass-neutral. But the callback's parameter bindings (`step`, `index`) become `for` bindings — roughly equal. So mass-neutral, clarity-improving (Rule 2).\n\nHmm, mass-neutral again. Both my candidates are mass-neutral. That's OK — Rule 2 trumps APP and naming/clarity improvements are valid even when mass-neutral.\n\nBut let me reconsider: can I find a genuinely mass-reducing improvement? Let me look hard.\n\n#### Mass-reducing candidate: `policyBasePremium` double-loop\n\nThe two loops over items are necessary (count-then-price). Can't easily merge without changing the algorithm. No.\n\n#### Mass-reducing candidate: `itemSurcharges` \n\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  let surcharge = 0;\n  if (item.cursed) surcharge += CURSE_RATE * base;\n  if ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD) surcharge += HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base;\n  return surcharge;\n}\n```\n\nCould be:\n```typescript\nfunction itemSurcharges(item: Item): number {\n  const base = itemBaseValue(item);\n  return (\n    (item.cursed ? CURSE_RATE * base : 0) +\n    ((item.enchantment ?? 0) >= HIGH_ENCHANT_THRESHOLD ? HIGH_ENCHANT_RATE * base : 0)\n  );\n}\n```\nThis replaces 2 ifs (8) + 2 += assignments (12) + let surcharge init... with 2 ternaries (8) + 1 + (2). Hmm: original has `let surcharge = 0` (binding 1 + assignment 6? or just init), 2 `if` (8), 2 `surcharge +=` (12). Total ~ 27+. Refactored: 2 ternary conditionals (8), `base` binding (1), 2 multiplications, 1 addition. Roughly: 8 (conditionals) + ... Actually `item.cursed ? CURSE_RATE * base : 0` — ternary (4) + CURSE_RATE (1) + base (1) + 0 (1) + * (invocation? 2). This is getting complicated. \n\nThe point: replacing `let` + `if` + `+=` with a pure expression reduces assignments (mass 6 each) which are expensive. `let surcharge = 0` (assignment 6 + binding 1 + constant 0 = 1) and two `surcharge += ...` (assignment 6 each = 12). That's ~19 in assignment mass alone. The expression form has no assignments, just 2 ternaries (8) and arithmetic. So mass drops significantly (by ~11+).\n\nBut does it improve clarity? The `if` version is arguably more readable than nested ternaries with arithmetic. Rule 2 vs Rule 4/APP. The `if` version is quite clear (each surcharge added conditionally). The ternary-expression version is more \"clever\" and less readable. Per \"Rule 2 trumps APP\" and \"Prefer explicit over clever code,\" I should keep the `if` version. So NOT a good refactoring — clarity would suffer.\n\nOK. So mass-reducing improvements tend to hurt clarity here. The code is already well-balanced.\n\n### Settled plan\n\nI'll do the **naming refactoring**: `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium`, with comment tightened. This is the clearest Rule 2 win (first priority), mass-neutral, safe (internal function).\n\nLet me also reconsider doing the `forEach` → `for...of` change. Actually, I'll keep it focused: just the rename, since that's the strongest single defensible improvement and the instructions say one at a time. Actually, let me reconsider whether the rename alone is \"enough.\"\n\nThe instructions: \"MUST attempt at least one refactoring (or document why none is possible).\" A rename IS a refactoring. And naming is the FIRST priority. So yes, the rename satisfies the mandate.\n\nBut let me make sure the rename is genuinely an improvement and not churn. `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium`:\n- The function returns the per-item base PREMIUM (for components: 25; for main items: their base premium). \n- The sibling `itemInsuranceValue` is specific (insurance). \n- \"value\" is generic/ambiguous; \"premium\" is precise.\n- The comment already calls it \"a single item's premium.\" So the name should match.\n\nYes, this is a genuine Rule 2 improvement. The name will now reveal that it returns a premium, matching its comment and usage.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider ONE thing. Is `itemBaseValue` perhaps intentionally named \"value\" because it's used as a generic base for BOTH premium calculation AND surcharge calculation? I.e., it's the \"base value\" of the item, used in multiple contexts? \n\nLooking at usages:\n1. `policyBasePremium`: `total += itemBaseValue(item)` — here it IS the premium being summed.\n2. `itemSurcharges`: `const base = itemBaseValue(item)` — here it's the surcharge BASE, which happens to equal the item's base premium.\n\nSo in both cases, the value is the item's base premium. In context 2, the premium is *used as* the surcharge base, but it IS the premium. So `itemBasePremium` is accurate in both. The \"value\" name obscures that it's specifically the premium. Rename is justified.\n\nAlright, let me also double check there are no other references to `itemBaseValue` elsewhere (e.g., in tests).\n\nLet me grep.\n\nActually, let me just do the rename and run tests. Let me grep first to be safe.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should I rename `itemBaseValue`'s comment? Current: \"Per-item base value: a single item's premium, before block discounts.\" New: \"Per-item base premium before block discounts.\" Shorter and matches the name.\n\nLet me now also compute the APP mass before/after for the file (for the rename, it's identical, but I'll document the overall file mass for completeness, and note the rename is mass-neutral but Rule 2-improving).\n\nActually, the instructions want before/after mass for the refactoring. For a pure rename, before mass == after mass. I'll state that clearly: the rename improves Rule 2 (reveals intent) at neutral APP mass, which is acceptable since Rule 2 trumps APP.\n\nHmm, but that feels like a weak refactoring report. Let me see if combining with the `forEach`→`for...of` (also Rule 2, also mass-neutral) makes it more substantive. Two clarity improvements. Both Rule 2. \n\nActually, let me reconsider the `forEach` change once more for whether it's truly an improvement or just style churn. \n\n`forEach` callback captures and mutates outer state — this is a recognized smell because `forEach`/`map`/`filter` suggest functional iteration, but side-effectful callbacks violate that expectation. A `for...of` loop explicitly signals imperative iteration. For a body that pushes to an external array, mutates a flag, and mutates a map, `for...of` is the honest choice. So yes, it's a legitimate Rule 2 improvement,