ssed: the `INSURANCE_VALUE = 10 × BASE_PREMIUM` consolidation. Let me reconsider the Rule 2 argument against it more rigorously, because if it's actually a domain rule, consolidating is a BIG DRY win (removes a whole table + constant + function branch duplication).\n\nThe spec comments:\n- \"Base premium per single main item type.\" → `BASE_PREMIUM`\n- \"Insurance value per single main item type; components are 250 G each.\" → `INSURANCE_VALUE`\n\nThese are described as two separate domain concepts with two separate tables. The spec author EXPLICITLY enumerated insurance values separately (1000, 600, 800, 400, 250). If the rule were \"insurance = 10× premium,\" the spec would likely state that rule rather than enumerate. The fact that it enumerates suggests they're independent specifications.\n\nMoreover, conceptually: \"base premium\" (what you pay periodically) and \"insurance value\" (max payout per item) are different domain ideas that an actuary would set independently. Their 10× alignment in this kata is likely a coincidence of the chosen round numbers, OR a simplification the author didn't intend to encode as a rule.\n\nCrucially: refactoring should not INTRODUCE a coupling that isn't established by tests. The tests don't establish \"insurance = 10× premium\" as a rule; they only happen to have values consistent with it. If I consolidate and a future test adds, say, a \"ring\" with premium 50 and insurance 700 (not 10×), my consolidation breaks. The current separate-table design is more robust to such additions.\n\nTherefore: KEEP separate. This is the disciplined, Rule-2-respecting decision. Document it.\n\nOK so my final plan:\n1. Rename `itemBaseValue` → `itemBasePremium` (Rule 2). Test.\n2. Convert `forEach` → `for...of` in `runScenario` (Rule 2: honest imperative signaling). Test.\n\nBoth mass-neutral, both clarity improvements. Document APP (neutral) and reasoning.\n\nWait, actually, I realize I should double check: does the rename `itemBaseValue` → `it