focus on the most relevant parts. The recent Green phase was about `processClaim`. Let me look at that function:\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nLet me think about this. There's a pattern here: each damage entry is processed and validated. There's a potential issue - there's a duplication of the \"find and remove from pool\" pattern that uses `findIndex` + `splice`. \n\nActually, let me think more carefully about what the most valuable refactoring would be.\n\nLet me look at the validation patterns across the codebase:\n\n1. In `processClaim`:\n   - `if (damage.amount < 0)` throw\n   - `const index = pool.findIndex(...)` + `if (index === -1)` throw + `pool.splice`\n\n2. There's an \"unknown item type\" validation done at quote time (`assertKnownItemTypes`), but at claim time the damage's `itemType` is checked against `policy.items` (not against known types).\n\nLet me think about whether there's a way to extract the validation logic. Actually, the claim-time validation is conceptually: validate each damage entry. We could extract a `validateDamage` or similar helper. But would that improve clarity?\n\nLet me focus on Rule 3 (DRY) and Rule 4 (Fewest Elements).\n\nLooking at `processClaim` more carefully:\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nThe loop body validates and processes each damage. There's a subtle thing: the two validations could be combined into a single helper `claimItemForDamage(pool, damage)` that finds and removes the item, throwing appropriate errors. This would improve cohesion by separating validation+extraction from accumulation.\n\nActually, let me think about what gives the most benefit. Let me calculate mass first.\n\n### Current `processClaim` mass:\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];                                          // binding (policy, incident, pool) + invocation [...spread?] + assignment\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;                                                  // binding + constant 0 + assignment\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {                                  // loop + binding + invocation\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {                                                // conditional + invocation (<)\n      throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);  // invocation + constant + binding\n    }\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);      // binding + invocation + binding (it) + invocation (===)\n    if (index === -1) {                                                     // conditional + invocation + constant\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);  // invocation + constant + binding\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);                                   // binding + invocation + constant\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;  // invocation + invocation + invocation + binding (DEDUCTIBLE) + assignment\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));  // binding + invocation + invocation + invocation + binding\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;                                            // assignment\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };                     // constant + binding\n}\n```\n\nThis is getting complex. Let me think about a cleaner refactor that would actually improve clarity.\n\nOne observation: the function does two things mixed together:\n1. Validation (negative amount, item covered)\n2. Processing (accumulate payout, consume items from pool)\n\nAnd it does the capping at the end.\n\nLet me think about extracting a helper `takeInsuredItem(pool, damage)` that validates the damage and returns the matched item (removing it from the pool). This separates the \"find & validate & consume\" concern from the \"accumulate\" concern.\n\nActually wait, let me re-read the loop:\n\n```typescript\nfor (const damage of incident.damages) {\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (index === -1) {\n    throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n  uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n}\n```\n\nThe validation of `damage.amount < 0` happens before finding the item. That's a bit odd — we validate the amount before checking the item is covered. But it doesn't matter functionally since either error throws. However, ordering could matter for which error message is shown when both conditions fail. The tests don't test this combination, so the order is a design choice. The current order: amount check first.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest extraction. I think extracting a helper `claimItem(pool, damage): Item` that does the validation + extraction would be clean:\n\n```typescript\n/** Validate a damage entry and remove one matching insured item from the pool. */\nfunction claimItem(pool: Item[], damage: Damage): Item {\n  if (damage.amount < 0) {\n    throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n  }\n  const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n  if (index === -1) {\n    throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n  }\n  const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n  return item;\n}\n```\n\nThen `processClaim` becomes:\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    const item = claimItem(pool, damage);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, this separates concerns but adds a function. Does it really improve? It does separate validation from accumulation, which is a cohesion improvement (Rule 2 Reveals Intent - the loop body becomes clearer about what it does: claim an item, accumulate payout).\n\nBut is it a clear win? Let me think about whether the original is more