he loop. Let me re-examine.\n\nThe loop computes `uncappedPayout` by summing `reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE` for each damage. The validation + extraction is scaffolding for getting `item`. \n\nExtracting `takeInsuredItem(pool, damage): Item` makes the loop say: \"for each damage, take the insured item it applies to, and add its reimbursed amount (minus deductible) to the payout.\" That's cleaner intent.\n\nI'll go with this. Let me also verify the tests will still pass — the behavior (error messages, order) is preserved exactly.\n\nWait, one concern: order of validation. Currently amount checked before item lookup. In my extraction, I preserve that order (amount check first, then findIndex). So behavior is identical. Good.\n\nLet me also double-check: does any test check that a negative amount is caught even when the item isn't in the pool? Looking at the negative-amount test:\n\n```typescript\nit(\"CLI: claim damage with negative amount -> exit non-zero\", () => {\n  const cli = runCli({\n    customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: 0 },\n    steps: [\n      { op: \"quote\", items: [{ type: \"sword\" }] },\n      {\n        op: \"claim\",\n        policy: 0,\n        incident: { cause: \"fire\", damages: [{ itemType: \"sword\", amount: -200 }] },\n      },\n    ],\n  });\n  expect(cli.exitCode).not.toBe(0);\n  expect(cli.stderr.length).toBeGreaterThan(0);\n  expect(cli.stdout).toBe(\"\");\n});\n```\n\nThe item \"sword\" IS in the policy here, so amount check triggers first (amount < 0). The test only checks exit non-zero + stderr + no stdout, not the specific message. So order preservation is safe regardless.\n\nOK let me now also think: is there a better name than `takeInsuredItem`? The pool contains the policy's insured items. \"take\" implies removal. `takeInsuredItem(pool, damage)` — \"take the insured item (from pool) that this damage refers to, validating the damage\". Reasonable. Alternatives: `claimItem`, `consumeInsuredItem`, `pullInsuredItem`. I