d\" refactoring that improves readability when the extracted block has a clear single purpose. I'm confident this is a genuine Rule 2 improvement.\n\nLet me write the refactored code.\n\nActually, hold on. Let me reconsider the mass to make sure I'm reducing or at least not increasing significantly, and document it. Let me carefully count.\n\nI'll count mass for `processClaim` only (the affected function) before and after, plus the new helper. Actually, the APP mass should be calculated for the changed region. Let me do the whole `processClaim` + helper.\n\n### Before: `processClaim`\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nCounting (I'll count each token):\n- `policy`, `incident` (params/bindings): 2 → 2\n- `const pool = [...policy.items]`: binding `pool` (1), spread invocation `[...]` — hmm, is `[...]` an invocation? Array spread/literal. I'll treat `[...policy.items]` as: invocation (policy.items property access? binding access). This is ambiguous. Let me simplify: `policy.items` is a binding access (mass 1 each? or 0?). Per APP, bindings are mass 1. `policy.items` — `policy` is a binding (1). `.items` access — treat as invocation (2)? Property access is typically not counted as invocation. Ugh, APP is fuz