e ADD the helper function definition with that same block (mass ~57) plus its own signature bindings.\n\nSo total mass across the file: the block mass (~57) now exists once (in the helper) instead of once (inline). The inline call is ~11. So total goes from 57 (inline) to 57 (helper body) + 11 (call site) + helper overhead (bindings for params). That's an INCREASE, not decrease, because the call site adds mass while the body stays.\n\nWait, that means extraction INCREASES total mass? Because the call site adds ~11 and the body is duplicated... no, the body MOVES, not duplicates. So: before = 57 (block inline). After = 57 (block in helper) + 11 (call). That's +11. Mass increases by ~11.\n\nHmm. So by APP mass, extraction is worse. But APP says Rule 2 (clarity) trumps mass. So if the extraction improves clarity enough, it's still justified.\n\nBut is it a clear clarity win? The loop body goes from ~6 statements to ~2 statements. That's a clarity win. But it's a modest one, and the function already had clear comments.\n\nHmm, I'm now less sure this is a good refactor. Increasing mass for modest clarity gain... Let me reconsider.\n\nLet me reconsider what the BEST refactor is. Maybe there's something that reduces mass AND improves clarity.\n\nLet me look again very carefully at `processClaim` and the rest.\n\nLooking at `runScenario`:\n```typescript\nfor (const [index, step] of scenario.steps.entries()) {\n  if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n    const items = step.items ?? [];\n    assertKnownItemTypes(items);\n    results.push({ premium: quotePremium(scenario.customer, items, hasPriorQuote) });\n    hasPriorQuote = true;\n    policies.set(index, { items, remainingCap: CAP_MULTIPLIER * insuranceSum(items) });\n  } else {\n    const policy = policies.get(step.policy);\n    if (!policy) throw new Error(`claim references unknown policy: ${step.policy}`);\n    results.push(processClaim(policy, step.incident));\n  }\n}\n```\n\nIn the quote branch, `const items = step.items ?? [];` defaults to empty. Then `assertKnownItemTypes(items)`, `quotePremium(..., items, ...)`, and `policies.set(index, { items, ... })`. `items` is used 3 times. Fine.\n\nThe `??` (nullish coalescing) — is that an operator counted? Under my rules, invocation (2). It's a conditional-ish operator though. Eh.\n\nLet me look at the rounding helpers:\n```typescript\nconst EPSILON = 1e-9;\nconst roundUp = (amount: number): number => Math.ceil(amount - EPSILON);\nconst roundDown = (amount: number): number => Math.floor(amount + EPSILON);\n```\nClean.\n\nLet me look at `policyBasePremium`:\n```typescript\nfunction policyBasePremium(items: Item[]): number {\n  let total = 0;\n  const componentCounts: Record<string, number> = {};\n  for (const item of items) {\n    if (isComponent(item.type)) {\n      componentCounts[item.type] = (componentCounts[item.type] ?? 0) + 1;\n    } else {\n      total += itemBasePremium(item);\n    }\n  }\n  for (const count of Object.values(componentCounts)) {\n    total += count === BLOCK_SIZE ? BLOCK_PREMIUM : count * COMPONENT_BASE;\n  }\n  return total;\n}\n```\n\nThis has two loops. The component counting could be done more simply. But it works. Mass is moderate. Not obviously improvable without changing approach.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider the `processClaim` extraction once more, but think about whether there's a way to reduce mass.\n\nWhat if instead of extracting a helper, I look at the validation differently? \n\nThe amount check `if (damage.amount < 0)` is a pre-condition on the damage. Could I validate all damages upfront? No, that changes the order of errors (currently, processing happens incrementally and an error mid-way... well, actually if an error throws, nothing is committed because `policy.remainingCap -= payout` only happens at the end, and `pool` is a local copy). So upfront validation wouldn't change observable behavior much, but it'd restructure. Not clearly better.\n\nAlternatively: Is there dead code or redundancy?\n\nLet me look at `isKnownItemType`:\n```typescript\nconst isKnownItemType = (type: string): boolean =>\n  BASE_PREMIUM[type] !== undefined || isComponent(type);\n```\nUsed in `assertKnownItemTypes`. Fine.\n\nLet me reconsider the whole file for the single most impactful, mass-reducing clarity-preserving refactor.\n\nHmm, what about `quotePremium`:\n```typescript\nfunction quotePremium(\n  customer: { yearsWithMHPCO: number },\n  items: Item[],\n  isFollowUp: boolean,\n): number {\n  const policyBase = policyBasePremium(items);\n  const surcharges = items.reduce((sum, it) => sum + itemSurcharges(it), 0);\n  const firstInsurance = policyBase * FIRST_INSURANCE_RATE;\n  const loyaltyDiscount =\n    customer.yearsWithMHPCO >= LOYALTY_THRESHOLD ? LOYALTY_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  const followUpDiscount = isFollowUp ? FOLLOW_UP_RATE * policyBase : 0;\n  return roundUp(\n    policyBase + surcharges + firstInsurance - loyaltyDiscount - followUpDiscount + PROCESSING_FEE,\n  );\n}\n```\n\nThis is clear and well-named. The explaining variables (`firstInsurance`, `loyaltyDiscount`, `followUpDiscount`) aid clarity. Mass is higher due to many bindings, but Rule 2 (clarity) justifies them. Not a refactor target.\n\nOK, let me step back. I need to make at least one refactoring. Let me pick the one with the best clarity-to-cost ratio.\n\nGiven my analysis, the `takeInsuredItem` extraction is a legitimate Rule 2 improvement (loop body becomes focused on payout accumulation, validation delegated to a named helper), even though mass slightly increases. Rule 2 trumps APP mass.\n\nBut I'm bothered by the mass increase. Let me see if there's a refactor that reduces mass.\n\nWait — actually, let me reconsider. Is there a simpler improvement: the `processClaim` does `const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);` after `findIndex`. This is the standard \"find then splice\" pattern. An alternative is to not pre-check and rely on splice... no, splice returns empty array if index is -1, so we'd lose the error. \n