ion is slightly surprising. Maybe the name should reflect validation. But that's hard. \n\nLet me reconsider: split into two concerns?\n- `assertDamageValid(damage)` — checks amount ≥ 0 (and could check itemType is known? no, that's separate)\n- `takeInsuredItem(pool, itemType)` — finds and removes\n\nBut that's TWO helpers, more elements (Rule 4). And `assertDamageValid` only checks one thing (amount). Over-extraction.\n\nI think one helper `takeInsuredItem(pool, damage)` that validates + extracts is the right granularity. The amount check is a precondition for claiming. It's fine.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing once more and consider NOT extracting, but instead a different improvement.\n\nLet me reconsider: is the current `processClaim` actually clear enough that extraction adds noise? Let me re-read:\n\n```typescript\nfunction processClaim(policy: Policy, incident: Incident): ClaimResult {\n  const pool = [...policy.items];\n  let uncappedPayout = 0;\n  for (const damage of incident.damages) {\n    if (damage.amount < 0) {\n      throw new Error(`damage amount must not be negative: ${damage.amount}`);\n    }\n    const index = pool.findIndex((it) => it.type === damage.itemType);\n    if (index === -1) {\n      throw new Error(`damage references item not covered by policy: ${damage.itemType}`);\n    }\n    const [item] = pool.splice(index, 1);\n    uncappedPayout += reimbursementRate(item) * damage.amount - DEDUCTIBLE;\n  }\n  const payout = roundDown(Math.min(uncappedPayout, policy.remainingCap));\n  policy.remainingCap -= payout;\n  return { payout, remainingCap: policy.remainingCap };\n}\n```\n\nIt's pretty clear. The loop does: validate amount, find item, validate found, consume item, accumulate. After the Green added the amount check, the loop is busier. \n\nI'll go with the extraction. It's a standard, defensible \"extract method\" that improves the loop's focus. Let me write it.\n\nActually, let me reconsider one more time if there's a